In article <Pine.GSO.4.10.9904291828250.5277-100000@garbo.nepean.uws.edu.au>,
Darren  <n9807471@scholar.nepean.uws.edu.au> wrote:
>I recently acquired a decstation 5000/20 and I need to know how to
>activate the network interface so that I can connect it to my other
>machine. The decstation came with openBSD installed on it. I have no idea
>what parts of openBSD were installed and have no way to install anything
>else on it. The 200Mb HDD has 5Mb free and there is no floppy drive. I
>found the source code for a driver for the network interface on the net,
>but do not know how to get it onto the Decstation or how to install it.

You could probably fairly easily install NetBSD on your DECstation.  The
device drivers for your network interface almost certainly are in the
OpenBSD distribution that's already installed on it, but I don't use
OpenBSD so I don't know how to help you.  The machine-dependent code in
the DECstation port of OpenBSD really hasn't been worked on, AFAICT, since
it was lifted from NetBSD about two major releases ago, so NetBSD is
probably a better choice.

See http://www.netbsd.org, then "supported platforms", then "DEC MIPS"
or "pmax".  You'll find a NetBSD distribution and instructions for
installing by several different methods.

The NetBSD 1.4 release is imminent -- there should be a NetBSD 1.4_BETA
snapshot available for your machine now and that's probably what you
want to install.

