In article <3728CA53.18B11AF1@mail.amsinc.com>, "A.J. Ewell" <a.j._ewell@mail.amsinc.com> writes:
:   trying to connect MMJ line from microVAX 3800 to a 9-pin COM1 on a
:pc.

  MicroVAX DB9 -> H8575-B to BC16E to H8571-J <- PC DB9
  MicroVAX MMJ -> BC16E to H8571-J <- PC DB9

:my real VAX (4000-106A) is connected to an old PS2 with a DB25 com port.
:the H8571-J looks sweet (PC/AT 9pin to MMJ).  have on hand an H8571-F,
:which worked at one time, but i see only 5 pins.  supposedly MMJ ports
:don't care about pin 1.

  On the DB25 and the MicroVAX DB9 9-pin pinout, pin one is a ground.

:  lacking an adapter i have a 9-to-25 modem cable and a
:female-female bender going to the (male) H8571-F (MMJ to DB25). 
:naturally it didn't work.  tried hookup from COM1 to a VT terminal.
:seeing no data at either end, either there's the Tx/Rx problem, or i 
:have a dropout somewhere.  TSIU, will the H8571-J allow a terminal 
:emulator to talk through a com port to the console, or does Tx/Rx need 
:to be switched. (why did all the breakout boxes get trashed?)

  The DECconnect wiring scheme has a crossover built into the BC16E 
  cabling -- DECconnect is similar to EIA-423 signaling.

  Check the OpenVMS FAQ -- there are two different and incompatible DB9
  9-pin pinouts in use: the 9-pin pinout used on PC systems, and the 
  MicroVAX 9-pin pinout, a pinout that predates the now-defacto PC 
  9-pin pinout.  The OpenVMS FAQ has the part numbers for most of the 
  adapters.

  The H8571-J is a PC DB9 female to MMJ straight-through adapter, while 
  the H8571-F is a DB25 male to MMJ adapter with cross-over.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




