In article <7g9dga$4oj$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, David Reid <reidd_28@my-dejanews.com> writes:

>I've just re-installed V4.0 OS onto an AlphaServer 1000. During the
>installation it asked which disklabel partition to use - the existing, or the
>one it recommended. I selected its recommendation. Although in hindsight I
>think I should have stuck with the existing because now when I try to newfs a
>file system on /dev/rz0 it complains about overlapping partitions.

/dev/rz0 isn't a device name that one normally hands to newfs, since
it doesn't include a partition letter.  I'm not sure what it tries
to do.  I'd expect it to fail since that device name doesn't exist,
but it could slip in a default partition and go from there.  If it
does, it could very easily be trying to to use a partition that
overlaps with an existing one; A or C.  In the case of A, that is
probably the root file system that it just kept you from overwriting.
In the case of C it was the whole disk that it kept you from over-
writing; all the file systems on that disk.

>Am I right in thinking it's something to do with the disklabel?

Vaguely.  Newfs most likely thought that it was trying to over-
write a partition that was in use (probably was right) and refused
to do so.  Your lucky day...

>If so, what's the solution? Can I recover back to the original
>disk partition label?

The solution is to use full device names so that you know what
it is trying to do.  Also, knowing which device it is trying to
use, you look at the label and see if overlaps any other
partitions that are being used.

