In article <3728D549.812860C4@amazing.com>,
David H Dennis <david@amazing.com> wrote:
> - Is a VAXStation 4000 VLC a worthwhile thing to own?
> - How big and heavy is it?  I searched the web in vain for
>   a picture/description.  All I could find is that it's about
>   6x the speed of the original VAX.

It's a pizza box, maybe 2" thick, and a few pounds.  It takes 6 4MB
SIMMs, and a 1" thick 3.5" SCSI drive (note the 1.6" thick drives are too
thick!)  There is NO place in the box for anything else.

It has a SCSI port for other SCSI widgets, and built-in ethernet and
video. It's SYNC_ON_GREEN and does not use a standard VGA connector.
I believe the display is fixed at 1280x1024 pixels.  It has a couple
built-in serial ports.  Also note the mouse is the old DEC corporate
mouse, not a PC mouse, even though the connector looks the same (it's not).

See www.montagar.com to see about getting a free VMS license.  As to CDROM
drives, you'll need an external SCSI drive and it must support 512 byte
sectors, which is not the PC standard.  Some (not all) PC drives also support
512 byte sectors.

-Mike



