> - Is a VAXStation 4000 VLC a worthwhile thing to own?

I see the high bids at around $50.  How can you go wrong at that price ?
They are also fully loaded (ahem) with 24Mb of memory, so in that respect
you are getting a complete system for the price.  The RZ25 disk will be
fine for getting VMS going, although you might want to look out for a 1Gb
alternative, or even up to 2Gb if you can get a cool-running (slower)
drive.

It's certainly the neatest and most portable Vax that DEC made.

> - Would a Sun 3/60 monitor (of which I happen to have a spare)
>   or a SGI Personal Iris model (of which ditto) work with the
>   beast?  Both monitors have separate R/G/B cables.

After much research (trust me), I identified 3 different graphics cards.
Unless someone has been swapping bits around, the model identification on
the back is a guide to what was factory installed.

VS48K-AA - 1024x768, 66(72?)Hz - RGB  (unknown connector, possibly 15-pin D)
VS48K-BA - 1024x768, 60Hz      - SVGA (miniature D, PC-style)
VS48K-CA - 1280x1024, 72Hz     - RGB  (DEC-special, 3 mini-coax)

(I'm a little unsure about the first, as we've not got one in the office)
The -BA version was typically supplied with a 13" monitor (DEC used to quote
viewable area), any decent 14/15" device will do.  The -CA version demands
the most from the monitor - even some brand new 17" multisync devices are
struggling to reach the required 130MHz bandwidth.  Note that you cannot
change the output of these cards in any way.

I can't help you with your Sun monitors.  Even when they used to use Sony
devices (as did DEC), there was a subtle model number variation which was
probably due to different internal tweaks to handle the different signal
timings.  If you know what you're doing, you can make the internal
adjustments yourself.  Otherwise, you need to get the right DEC monitor,
or use a modern multisync device with suitable bandwith and sync-on-green
support.

