alexander fohringer wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> I need to connect several terminals (spread over the whole floor) to the
> network. Can I use a DECServer 900TM
> for this and if do I need any additional components (how is this Server
> connected to the LAN??).
> Thanks for any information - it's quite urgent - I need this information
> for a project on university.
> 
> Kind regards
> 
> FOHRINGER Alexander

The DecServer 900TM is a module which requires a means of powering it
and 
connecting it to the network. There are two choices: A Hub One, which
fits 
a single module, or a Hub900 which will take up to 8.

The Hub One will need a transceiver for the type of media you use,
probably
10BASET or 10BASE2 if I had to guess. Either buy the Dec ones, which
will
fit the restricted space, or buy the smallest you can find and hope they 
will fit.

The Hub 900 will require a repeater in one of its 8 slots to run the
network 
connection - we usually use Repeater 90FA for this. It comes with one
PSU
and can take up to 3 more. One will run quite a lot of modules (usually
more 
than 4) but a second one gives redundancy. Price I think is around
$US3000,
so only worth it if you are using high densities of connections.

You may find using several DecServer 90M modules in stack adapters works
out
cheaper. They have a built-in 10BASE2 connector. The Decserver
literature
raves on about it being an Access Server (RAS etc) and omits to mention
that it is also a bog standard terminal server. It needs a load host,
whereas the 900TM is standalone I believe.

The load host can be a PC with Bootp, or a Vax or Alpha with MOP.

-- 
Alan Adams



