Karl N. Matthias wrote in message ...
> I'm a newish Digital/Tru64 UNIX admin though I'm quite experienced
>on Solaris and various BSD's.  I'm running into something which I have
>rarely seen on other systems.  When I run large apps or long compiles (ie
>push the memory limit) I get the following:
>
>swap space below 10 percent free
>
> This is strange to me since I have about 128 MB of swap and 128 MB

1)I guess, minimal swap space is size of RAM.

2)I advise you to make the size of swap equals 3 times of the RAM. This
configuration is easy to use with "paging" algorithm of virtual memory.
"Paging" is standard algorithm for Digital UNIX.

3)It's the most usable configuration of swap for Digital UNIX. If you use
memory-hungry applications, such as Oracle, SAP, BAAN, you should increase
swap up to 4 or even 5 times. It's required when "swapping" algorithm was
used.

4) Also, you can choose from two modes of virtual memory: Lazy and Eager.
Lazy mode doesn't reserve any swap space before downloading memory pages to
disk.
Eager mode reserves swap space by virtual memory allocation request. Read
the docs for tuning guidelines.


