Subject: Re: Q: Running out of swap
Date: 1999/04/29
Message-ID: <7g9pup$481@web.nmti.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 472329140
References: <Pine.GSO.3.96.990428231519.27106A-100000@odin>
Organization: Bailey Network Management
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

>swap space below 10 percent free

>	This is strange to me since I have about 128 MB of swap and 128 MB
>of RAM.  Even the biggest compiles or apps I run shouldn't be coming close
>to this limit!

If you're using "greedy swap", then it preallocates swap space for everything
in physical memory. To switch to "lazy swap" delete /sbin/swapdefault, which
is a symlink to the partition that you want to back memory with.

By the way, don't listen to anyone who tells you to allocate X times real
memory to swap partitions. Figure out how much VM you will need, allocate
that much space, and add 1x real memory if you're using greedy swap. RAM
is basically a cache for swap space, and having more RAM than one of these
algorithms says you "should" have just means you'll get a better hit ratio,
so long as you have enough swap in the first place.

-- 
