Please, please, please do yourself and Compaq a favour and don't 
publish this page again, its embarrasing rubbish.

The 2 CPU DS20 number is under 50% of the performance and much more
expensive than a 4 CPU XEON it does not look that good 
against a Sun E450 with 4 CPU's which is ~60% faster 
and ~50% cheaper per TPM using the same Sybase DBMS. The DS20
hardware config was a whopping 100,000 more expensive 
than the E450, if you add in both systems 5 year maintenance 
then the DS20 is even worse at 172,000 more expensive 
which begins to make it look like it should be gold plated.

The TPC-D result is old and Sun, HP, NCR and IBM all have 
much faster numbers than the ones on this page. Don't 
bother updating it though it won't look good. On the 
300 GB TPC-D Compaqs latest Number is ~50% of HPS best
and 22% of NCRs best. 

SAP's per CPU is a meaningless metric to SAP customers 
who are only interested in the number of SAPS your system
can do and Sun, HP and IBM all have systems that can 
do many many more SAPS.

The Baan numbers are also out of date a familiar story,
both HP and Sun have announced larger numbers with simular 
number os CPUs, but again BaaN BRU's per CPU are a 
meaningless measure to a BaaN customer and again both 
Sun and HP's Baan BRU numbers are larger than Compaqs.

The SPECweb numbers are selected for a 2 CPU system but as 
in the SAP and Baan numbers SPECweb is a measure of 
throughput. Sun, SNI and IBM all do bigger workgroup servers 
with more CPUs than the DS20 and they all deliver better 
SPECweb thoughput.

Probably the best example of what a crock this page is 
can be illustrated by the Compag GS140 which on SPECweb
has a much worse SPECweb/CPU or whatever meaningless metric 
Compaq would like to dream up than IBM, SNI or Suns
but Compaq didn't publish that number did they.

