In article <37214069.2338137@news.pangea.ca>, kkritsilas@bigfoot.com (Kostas Kritsilas) wrote:
>1. I take it the media for Open VMS 7.x is CD-ROM? If so, do I need
>to find a DEC CD-ROM, or is any CD-ROM usable? I have an external
>Plextor CD-ROM drive available, will this do? Is the special

You need a drive that supports 512 byte blocks rather than the PC style 
2048(?). I don't think the cable is a DEC-special but I've never needed to 
go looking anywhere else ...

>VAXstation 3100 (68 pin) to Centronics type SCSI (50 pin) cable
>available anywhere other than DEC/Compaq ?
>

>2. Perhaps this should have been asked first: Is the M76 compatible
>with Open VMS 7.x? The system has 24MB of Ram, and only a 200MB
>(RZ-24) hard drive. Upgrades to either required?

I ran a VS3100-76 with DECwindows and 32MB of memory (which is the maximum). 
Worked reasonably well (I was happy with it at the time but I now have a 
VS4000-90 ...)

The 200MB drive is seriously small these days. As you are going to be running 
a recent OpenVMS release which is quite tolerant of non-DEC SCSI disks, I 
would consider picking up a 1GB cheap SCSI drive. (I manage to run some of my 
test systems on 159MB RD54s, so I know how little room is left for anything 
useful). Be aware that the VAXstation 3100 series all have a ROM limitation 
that prevents reliable booting of OpenVMS from disks bigger than 1.073GB - 
data disks are fine; so keep a 1GB drive as a system disk and get one of those 
shiny new 50GB drives as a data disk!

I would also be wary of putting an hot-running (7200rpm +) drives in the 
system enclosure - RZ29Bs for example will melt in short order (I am told).

