>>Pushing SS:ESP on the stack during ring changes when
>>DEC style per-ring SPs would have avoided the need?
>
>What is the benefit of several SPs as opposed to several sets of GPRs?

Multiple GPRs would be OK, except that the 80x86 doesn't have them.  Some
PDP-11s do though, and they really aren't that useful.  My point was, IRET is
an atrocity, what could have been just a simple two-pops-and-go on a clean
architecture (i.e. the PDP-11!) turns into this complicated mess that has to
inspect the popped PSW and decide what else to pop, possibly including ES:ESP
itself, and maybe the rest of the segment regs (if a V86 task was interrupted).

>> No way to enable I/O instructions to non-ring-0 tasks w/o an 8 KB bitmap?
>
>Hmm.. I fail to see a problem with that.

Pointless waste of memory.  I don't care how cheap memory is, there's no
reason to just piss it away for fun.

>> No concept of the CPU's own interrupt priority so it all has to be
>> faked using external ICUs?
>
>Hmm.. what good are interrupt priorities anyways?

On a system with a total joke of an I/O system, it doesn't matter.  But putting
that aside, PC device drivers have to do extra work nursing the ICUs along
so they know what's going on (they don't even do IRET sniffing like the Z80
chips did).  Which would be only mildly annoying (2 or 3 extra instructions
plus a push/pop in every ISR), except in cases where you're chaining multiple
ISRs off the same vector (which wouldn't be necessary if vectored interrupts
had been done correctly in the PC architecture -- this one is IBM's fault
for using the chips wrong, Intel's not directly to blame since you *could*
use all 224 non-reserved vectors for hardware interrupts if you built your
own ICU) it gets confusing where you have to make sure that each individual
ISR doesn't send the EOI when it's the root ISR dispatcher's job, but then
again maybe it does after all if the ISR's *aren't* chained.

It's just more clutter, which the PDP-11 avoided entirely by planning
ahead a little.  And, if you actually *wanted* to share a vector between
two devices for some reason, the PDP-11 would let you do it from day one
with no special hardware.

>>  No SP-relative index mode (until later models) so HLL subroutine entry/exit
>>code has to be bloated up with setting up BP?
>
>Is it not what .jsr and .jret did anyways?

Don't know what macro package that is, but on a PDP-11 it's perfectly possible
for a compiler to keep track of how the SP has been adjusted so far since
entering the routine, and figure out the right offset to just index off SP to
wherever the locals and/or call parms are located.  No frame pointer needed.
That's how Small-C works, and it's the stupidest compiler around!

>Dunno... I never liked proliferation of instruction sets in PDP-11.
>FPP vs. FIS, mtps/mfps (returning result on stack, no less).
>The jewel of PDP-11 kludges was rtt of course.

Talk about scraping the bottom of the barrel!  Meanwhile the 80x86 has half
a dozen IMUL instructions, *no way* of reaching into another job's context
w/o mapping it manually, and gobs of instructions that are hardwired to
particular registers (so you have to constantly do a little dance to keep your
register values out of each other's way), constantly changing rules about
which instructions are fast and which are slow, and a handful of pointless
new instructions added to each new generation.  Why was BSWAP so important
that it had to be added to the *fourth* generation chip?  And I love the
fact that CPUID in general and the serial number feature in particular came
so late in the game, since both ideas were ripe for plucking from the PDP-11
architecture back in the 8086 days.  And would it have killed them to put
an invalid opcode trap, like every PDP-11 has, in those early chips?

Anyway if you want to talk about proliferation, you can't beat Intel for that.
With the release of the Pentium it was decided that we were supposed to
settle into the "RISC core" instructions, all the simple one-cycle stuff that
pairs nicely.  So we're supposed to pretend it's practically a RISC chip,
with significant performance penalties for using the genuinely *useful*
instructions like REPE CMPSB.  Then, they shovel that MMX crap onto the
architecture and go through a particularly stupid ad campaign trying to get
anyone to care (I'll bet that to this day most C compilers can go all day
long w/o generating a single MMX instruction as object).  It's just a huge
cluttered mess.

>In any case, a used program on 8086 could access a meg, as opposed
>to 2x64K on a high end PDP-11. This is what was its downfall.

But, the 8086 couldn't go past a meg *total*, while PDP-11s could handle 4 MB.
So 80x86 code did all those flips and twists with EMS and 80286 segmentation,
which was a nightmare.  Each time around, Intel adds just a handful more
address bits, so it takes kludges on top of kludges to make it all work now
(the P6 jump from 32 to 36 bits was particularly pointless).  Not that the
PDP-11 isn't guilty of the same thing, but it was ahead at the time the 8086
came out, and at least its bits are next to each other!

>> autodecrement addressing that works backwards...
>
>huh?

STD / LODSB decrements *after* the memory reference, which is rarely what
you actually want.  So you often get messy with subtracting 1 (2? 4?) from
your pointer before doing your byte/word/dword string instruction(s), and
then adding it back when you CLD again.

>>  It's a bigger mess than anything DEC ever built!
>
>Dunno... I never was too excited about PDP-11. Actually I hated to feed
>paper tapes into it from the outset. :)

Somehow, I doubt feeding paper tapes into an Intel box would be any better!
Anyway the thing about the PDP-11 architecture is that it's balanced and
it makes sense.  I've been writing 80x86 assembly language for *16 years*
and I *still* have to pull out the manuals pretty often to find my way around
those bizarre bit fields and nasty data structures and crazy privilege rules,
there's way too much cruft in there to keep it straight in my head.

John Wilson
D Bit



