I sure hope you do not seriously believe that.  Especially the OEM
manufacturers change their parts and setups so often that any
application benchmark you did on last weeks machine is bogus on
today's.  Sure, the chipset is the same, the CPU is the same (the DRAM
is most likely not), so your typical microbenchmark will tell you all
is cool.  But then your application still does not work as expected.
SPEC _is_ an application benchmark (with cooked applications, but
nonetheless) it sure as hell is affected by those changes in subtle
and not so subtle ways.  At least Dell is benchmarking real systems (I
think Intel is the only SPEC licensee who has that handwaving
clearance to test boards/processors rather than systems).  Anyone
swayed by the Alder board results and not figuring out where they came
from was most likely sorely disappointed by the results of the systems
they could actually buy at the time.


