In article <19990426183838.09527.00001894@ng27.aol.com>, rwatson767@aol.com (RWatson767) writes:
:I have a VAX station with a model number of VS42A-BW and don't know what it is.
:It has lots of connectors and a power connector but being painfull ignorant of
:Digital things have no knowledge of this item Any knowledge would be
:appreciated. .

  The VAXstation 3100 is a series of older VAX systems, capable of
  running current versions of OpenVMS VAX (see the hobbyist license 
  program referenced in the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions if
  you want to look into this).

  The systems were usually -- though not always -- packaged with 
  integrated SCSI and Ethernet capabilities, and all had graphics 
  capabilities.

  Before you try using a random PC monitor, please see the many
  previous discussions of "synch-on-green" requirements, and see
  the references around using older DIGITAL monitors on PC boxes.

  The members of the series were the models 30 and 40, the models
  38 and 48, and the model 76.  The 30 and 40 differed only in the
  size of the enclosure -- the model 30 has two rows of louvers
  on the side and three internal storage bays, the model 40 had
  three rows of louvers and five internal bays.  The same holds
  for the (somewhat faster) model 38 and 48 pair.  The model 76 
  was the fastest.  Based on remembrances of the part numbers, you 
  probably have a model 30 or 40.

  The OpenVMS FAQ will have some other references, and the archives
  at www.dejanews.com will have a bazzillion references to this 
  series of systems.

  The OpenVMS FAQ is available via a link at:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/

  The OpenVMS FAQ has a pointer to a MicroVAX hardware FAQ site.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




