In article <3726243A.A8A5EA5B@idcomm.com>, rickc@idcomm.com wrote:
>Depending on vintage and internal upgrades, it would be
>a VaxStation 3100 model 30 with either a KA45-AA cpu
>module or KA42-AA cpu module (the latter being a faster
>cpu). There may be dual scsi, i/o, network and other options
>installed in the box. I would need the part #'s from the
>modules to tell you what all you have, I.E. the KA45 cpu
>is a 54-20654-01 module & the KA42 could any one of three
>different part #'s.


I think you are mixing up early VAXstations and middling MicroVAXes.

The VAXstation 3100 Model 30 used a KA42-A to give about 2.8VUPs
The VAXstation 3100 Model 40 was a Model 30 in a bigger box.
The original mainboard was 54-17312-01. The later one was 54-19356-02 
(basically the same board as the M38 but with some slower components fitted - 
presumably done for cheapness of manufacturing).
The part numbers were VS42A for M30 and VS42S for M40.

The VAXstation 3100 Model 38 used a KA42-B to give about 3.8VUPs.
The VAXstation 3100 Model 48 was a Model 38 in a bigger box.
The mainboard was 54-19356-01
The part numbers were WS42A for M38 and WS42B for M48.

The MicroVAX 3100 Model 30 used a KA45-A to give about 5.0VUPs.
The MicroVAX 3100 Model 40 was a Model 30 in a bigger box.
The mainboard was indeed 54-20654-01.
The part numbers were something like 450ZN for M30 and 450ZM for M40.

