>Zalman Stern <zalman@netcom.com> wrote:
>>
>>Languages with exception handling pretty much eliminate the desire to do
>>the above. Given that this technology has been in common use for more than
>>a decade, you'd think it would be more or less adopted by now...
>
>  The languages I know with exception handling (C++, Java) have been 
>designed sufficiently poorly that lots of people still don't want to 
>use them.  

Having spent a lot of time with languages in which you can use a
"functional" approach, and have function return big, juicy, rich, complex
things, I've always disliked the C style in which any possibility of
returning anything useful through a function value is preempted by the
need of using the function value as a mechanism for signalling exceptions. 

So when I started to use C++, I used exception handling all over the
place... and, IF I understand correctly, I've thoroughly screwed myself by
doing so.  However, enlightenment on this would be welcome. 

IF I understand correctly, the gotcha--one that Stoustrup's book doesn't
explain or warn you against--is that the compiler code generated for
exception handling is not standardized and not necessarily interoperable,
either with code generated by C compilers or code generated by _other_ C++
compilers.  Therefore, you cannot use exception handling at all unless you
can guarantee that _all_ code in your program was generated by the same
C++ compiler.  

In my case: I write "front-end" code; the code calls a library, which is
provided by the company we are OEM-ing from; the library in turn makes
callbacks to "back-end" routines that I provide.  The library is
documented as if were written in C, and their code examples are in C. 
But, silly me, I thought that backward compatibility with C was almost
C++'s whole reason for being, and that I was reasonably safe mixing and
matching as long as I used extern "C" directives properly in my headers. 
I _did_ wonder why the program crashed whenever one of my callback
routines threw an exception... 


-- 
Daniel P. B. Smith
dpbsmith@world.std.com



