Daniel P. B. Smith (dpbsmith@world.std.com) wrote:
: In my case: I write "front-end" code; the code calls a library, which is
: provided by the company we are OEM-ing from; the library in turn makes
: callbacks to "back-end" routines that I provide.  The library is
: documented as if were written in C, and their code examples are in C. 
: But, silly me, I thought that backward compatibility with C was almost
: C++'s whole reason for being, and that I was reasonably safe mixing and
: matching as long as I used extern "C" directives properly in my headers. 
: I _did_ wonder why the program crashed whenever one of my callback
: routines threw an exception... 

You must wrap all callback routines with code that catches all exceptions
and turns them into error returns. This should be obvious in the case of a
callback routine that returns an error code. The compiler could not
possibly automagically turn a thrown exception into the right kind of error
code return.

You can experiment with writing templates to do some of the wrapper work
for you, but its probably not worthwhile. (Unless you know you have a
design that works and know all the C++ compilers you're targeting have
decent template support.) Probably better to either write a script to
preprocess the code or just do it by hand if there aren't many routines.

The binary compatibility gotcha is a big lose. It means that even if folks
could be convinced to use a richer calling convention for standard system
services and libraries (which I doubt will happen), different compilers on
the same platform would have to be modified to conform to the same binary
calling convention.

Another place where callback functions to C libraries lose is in type
checking and C++ style. In C++ you probably want an object which has a
method invoked when the callback is called. Once again, you can often get
this effect in a wrapper.

On the other side, you're going to need wrappers to call into the library
which check the error code and possibly throw an exception.

By the way, I don't advocate this technique in all situations. It really
depends on the details of the application and how much code lives on each
side of the abstraction boundaries.

-Z-



