In comp.arch Zalman Stern <zalman@netcom.com> wrote:

> There is definitely room for improvement in binary interoperability in C++
> environments.

The problems are not restricted to differences between C++
compilers and versions, but are also made worse by the normal
recommended C++ programming idiom where the sizes of all
objects are exposed through the .h files to the users of your
libraries who will put them on the stack, make arrays of them,
subclass them and generally do things that mean you can never
ever add as much as one single private member variable without
breaking binary compatibility with your old library versions.

There are ways around this 'fragile base class' problem, of
course, where you put all the data in a separate object, or
make sure you have enough padding in your classes for future
expansion, but C++ doesn't actively support this way of doing
things, or check that you don't accidentally get it wrong.

Qt and Be's libraries suffer from this.  I imagine any C++-based
library will.

