Is this a joke???  Pushing SS:ESP on the stack during ring changes when
DEC style per-ring SPs would have avoided the need?  No way to enable I/O
instructions to non-ring-0 tasks w/o an 8 KB bitmap?  No concept of the CPU's
own interrupt priority so it all has to be faked using external ICUs?  No
SP-relative index mode (until later models) so HLL subroutine entry/exit
code has to be bloated up with setting up BP?

Intel could have far lot worse than implement a PDP-11 with a bigger
process address space.  And sure enough they did!  The MMU was really the
only thing holding the PDP-11 back, Intel's replacement wasn't much better
(overall it actually addresed *less* memory until they did their horribly
kludged upgrades later, and even now A31:24 aren't contiguous with A23:0 in
the segment descriptors, what a botch job) and they poisoned a lot of the
rest of the architecture.  FPP faults that are transmitted ... somehow ...
a "halt" instruction that doesn't halt, autodecrement addressing that works
backwards...  It's a bigger mess than anything DEC ever built!  One thing
DEC knew how to do (in the old days) was choosing a reduced set of features
for a low-end machine, w/o crippling its usefulness (i.e. they chose the
*right* 1/3 of the architecture).  Intel skipped a whole lot of important
stuff (decent addressing modes, streamlined interrupt architecture), so that
they'd have room for a ridiculous mishmash of decimal instructions no one
uses, inaccurate trig functions, and other crap.  They didn't even keep
the very handy conditional call/return instructions of the 8080!

John Wilson
D Bit



