>(1 whole megabyte!).  The segment register scheme was also intended to
>facilitate dynamic relocation of code and data blocks within the main memory,
>which was a real problem for multi-tasking systems lacking virtual memory.
>
>Later x86 generations simply carried along the segment registers
>for compatibility.

  Actually, the segments in the 8086 have nothing in common with the 
"protected mode" segments in the 80386.  The former were simply imple-
mented as bits appended to the high end of the address word.  The latter
are more like MULTICS segments (except cleverly lobotomized to be much
less useful).  16-bit MULTICS-style segments were introduced in the i286
because Intel speculated that lots of distributed little objects would 
be the wave of the future, and these were extended to 32 bits in the 
i386 (by adding more base and magnitude bits before the i286 segment 
descriptor, to help maintain backwards-compatibility (pun intended) 
with i286 code).

  -- TTK




