In article <371EECA6.CEE1826E@uk.sun.com>,
andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com <andrew.harrison@uk.sun.nospam.com> wrote:
>
>But this is the whole point. Compaq have been marketing Alpha 
>almost exclusively on its SPECint/SPECfp performance, but very 
>few people buy systems to re-run SPEC, they do however buy 
>machines to do OLTP.

  There are plenty of companies which build big servers for 
running GCC, and many web servers are bottlenecked on their
perl CGI scripts.  Both GCC and perl are part of SPEC's int95
testsuite.

>Well do you really think that Compaq can compete with Alpha in 
>the sub 1000 dollar, no margin PC market, where being good enough 
>at a price point is the buying criteria. It certainly doesn't appeal
>to the likes of SGI.

  Oh, and SGI has just been the pinnacle of cosmic wisdom.  Not that
I disagree -- SGI probably shouldn't get into the commodity market.
They have nothing to offer there.

  Compaq, on the other hand, has a good thing going with the 21164PC
and third-party motherboard manufacturers.  The 21164PC is very small,
mostly (iirc) because they shrunk the cache.  This makes it all but 
useless for many corporate purposes because it will spend most of its
clock cycles starving for memory access, but well-suited to many home 
user applications which have extremely small working sets (qv Intel's 
Celeron outperforming Intel's PII for some applications (like Quake) 
despite a smaller cache, due to its faster-clocked cache).  Shrinking 
the 21164PC fab process should hike its clock speed to the 800MHz or
1GHz range, and lower its manufacturing cost to the point that it can
compete favorably with the x86 processors.  They have also been very
successful in convincing several (14 was the number in the press re-
lease I remember) motherboard manufacturers to build cheap Alpha 
boards suitable for the home market.  These motherboards take standard 
PC peripherals and memory.

  About a year ago, it was possible to buy a 533MHz 21164PC-based
computer system for about $1400, about $900 of which was just the 
CPU and motherboard.  The shrunk CPU should be much cheaper, and so
should the motherboards (though I have not checked on them lately).
I wouldn't be surprised to see $999 800MHz AlphaPC systems by the 
end of the year (though I also wouldn't be too surprised to see that
Compaq "doesn't get it", and tries to sell these systems for $1500
when they could sell them for $999 .. we'll see how it plays out).
Such a thing would be easily twice as fast as a 666MHz PIII at 
floating-point intensive (ie, multimedia) applications.  On the other 
hand, if the sub-$500 market subsumes the sub-$1000 market, there 
might not be room in consumerland for Alpha after all.

>Because OLTP numbers sell systems, SPECint/SPECfp numbers arn't as good 
>a weapon for sales people and sadly selling systems is the name of the 
>game if Alpha is to survive.

  It's been my direct experience that most people in a position to 
make buying decisions have a blind faith in benchmark numbers, and 
many of them put that faith in SPEC.  Whether this is a good or bad 
thing I'll leave up to individual bias.

>Take a step back, Digital and now Compaq are marketing Alpha systems 
>on their CPU performance (SPECint and SPECfp) and that is precisely 
>the problem. If I was a customer I would be confused, on one hand 
>Compaq tell me that they have the fastest CPUs and that Alpha is the
>worlds fastest micropocessor, on the other hand they cannot come up 
>with any examples of how this CPU advantage translates into better 
>performance for the applications that the bulk of Compaqs prospective 
>customers run. 

  They had an excellent demonstration booth set up at the last ESC,
including a dual-processor 21264 system running Quake under emulation
at very high graphics resolution, sans any acceleration hardware.  It
was smoother and faster than anything I'd ever seen on an Intel box
that *did* use acceleration hardware.  Sneer if you like, but the kind
of demonstration that people can see and get a gut feeling for is 
arguably at least as important as hard cold benchmark numbers.

  That aside, Compaq can easily come up with examples of how this CPU
advantage translates into better performance for the applications that
the bulk of Compaq's prospective customers run.  Development shops can
run GCC faster than ever; web servers can more perl CGI's faster; cir-
cuit manufacturers can run circuit emulations in a fraction of the time 
they would run on the systems they're using now.  These are just what I
can pull off the top of my head from what I have direct experience with
in the market (at Cygnus I worked with customers who had to throw a lot
of iron at running GCC so that they could run their builds in less than
a day, and my father is an IC engineer who I have helped find better
solutions for his time-consuming circuit emulation problem).  I'm sure 
someone whose actual job it is to find these demonstrations could do 
better.

>Could it be that a sucessfull architecture from a performance standpoint
>is a combination of CPU, system design, OS, applications software etc 
>or would this be too hertical a view to put forward on comp.arch. 

  I think you're right.  This is why Sun and Intel/Microsoft have done
so well in the market.  Compaq has the CPU and the system design.  They
have been working hard (and Digital before them) bolstering their OS and
applications software, and they have accomplished a tremendous deal.  
They have also geared up a serious marketing effort in the form of their
"Alpha Processor Inc" subsidiary, which should be making their move soon,
once all the pieces are in place (the shrunk 164PC, the new 264 systems,
the Alpha/K7 systems, Win64, the motherboard manufacturer alliances, etc).

>Lets be more specific, for Alpha to be a sucess it has to demonstrate 
>performance that is better than Intel and its other competitors for 
>commercial applications. This means, databases(DSS/DW/OLTP), messaging
>systems, mail servers, groupware servers, authentication systems, http 
>servers, file and print, firewalls etc. Compaq have not done this.

  I'm not sure what you mean by "demonstrate" here.  Do you mean that
they haven't publicly announced formal benchmark results covering these
applications?  Because you or I or anyone else can purchase an Alpha
(or in my case play with the one at work), run our applications, and see
for ourselves that the Alpha is very, very fast.  I would guess that the 
formal announcement of this will come when Alpha Processor Inc makes its 
big entrance.

>Given Alphas small and fragmented software portfolio, being spread 
>across NT, Tru64 and OpenVMS the only advantage that Compaq has is 
>the "performance" of their systems. This is what Compaq and Digital 
>before them have failed significantly to do. Except with SPECint and
>SPECfp which as you quite rightly point out do virtually no I/O 
>and are not necessarely a good predictor of for example OLTP
>performance.

  "Small and fragmented software portfolio"?  This is starting to
sound like FUD to me.  You make it sound like availability on WNT,
Tru64, and VMS is a *bad* thing.  Why didn't you mention AlphaLinux
in there too?

  Upon rereading what I've written, I realize it reads a lot like an 
advocacy post.  I apologize for that, but I think when the sole pur-
pose of a post is to contradict the FUD of another post, sounding 
like an advocate is unavoidable.

  -- TTK




