In article <5JX6Z1f.pudge@delphi.com>, Pudge <pudge@delphi.com> writes:
:Those Damn TORX screws,,,,,,,,,,,,hate those things.

  The guys that only had the flat-blade screwdrivers said the same 
  thing when the phillips heads came out.  :-)

  I acquired a set of Torx wrenches (from Tech America) for use on some
  Apple Macintosh systems I deal with.  They work quite nicely, and they
  can even get into the deep recess where those particularly tiny Torx 
  screws are located.

  While I don't specifically know why the Torx was chosen for use on the
  Apple or Compaq systems, my suspicion is that ease of manufacturing
  was one.  I expect that another is an attempt to discourage folks 
  from poking around inside the box, and to correspondingly reduce the
  chances of physical or static-related damage, damage that could result
  in extra support calls or in warrantee work.

  The design of computers is changing -- as the level of integration
  continues to increase, the need to access to the internals or the
  motherboard is reduced.  I would fully expect the need to access the 
  internals of a computer will be largely eliminated in the near future 
  -- much like the computers used in in vehicles, I would expect some 
  models of computers will be little more than epoxy-filled boxes.  (No, 
  I know of no plans to do that here at Compaq.)  Computers are heading 
  closer to toasters, stereos, or other appliances in design, and have 
  been for quite some time.

  Much like the evolution of amateur radio, there will certainly remain 
  a contingent of folks that like working inside computers or that have 
  various technical and/or business reasons to do so.  That won't be 
  (isn't) the mass market audience, however.


