Greg Lindahl (lindahl@pbm.com) wrote:
: So your excuse for repeatedly not bothering to explain what you are
: talking about is one posting by someone else? I've complained 4x now
: that you don't specify what you're talking about, and you continue to
: do it.

Actually, many of us are understanding his point just fine. (Whether we
agree with it or not...) What your problem is, I don't know.

I still feel that the 21264's poor showing on database benchmarks is
unexplained. It should have an advantage there too. (As has been pointed
out, these benchmarks are often CPU bound. And if they aren't, they can be
made CPU bound.)

: I don't run PRO-E. Why do you think PRO-E is more like my applications
: than SPEC95fp?

Ain't what he was saying. What he was saying is that PRO-E sells hardware
and is a datapoint. It would be a better post if the performance numbers
were included, but I didn't have any problem understanding his point. To
some extent this comes down to an argument between synthetic benchmarks
vs. application ones. If the 21264 is lacking on application benchmarks,
but showing incredibly well on SPEC, then it demonstrates something wrong
with the chip or with the system its used in. Or the inapplicability of
SPEC. Greg apparently has some counter examples and might do better to
provide data than to dismiss the whole discussion as marketing fluff.

It is no great revelation to anyone who has done performance tuning that
the best chip imaginable often won't deliver an end user performance
improvement that is interesting. Call it heartbreak or whatever. What is
interesting is to know why this is true for particular cases.

-Z-



