Sandy Kirby <sandy.kirby@unforgettable.com> writes:
> I just acquired a Decserver 200/MC & was wondering about using it to
> dial-into my BAN (Basement Area Network).
>
> Can anyone point me to sources of info. on the net for the 200/MC?

  This is a common question. Someone should FAQ it (if it isn't already).

  A DS200 is a low-end box that only speaks LAT (a proprietary DEC protocol).
It needs a load host that speaks MOP to load it (but free MOP implementations
are available). It is limited to speeds of 19200 baud and lower, and has no
routing capability. [The also-obsolete DECrouter 250, which supported DECnet,
uses the same box with a different boot ROM and additional memory.] You also
need the load software (from DEC).

  There's no reason someone couldn't write a TCP/IP stack/load image for the
DS200, except that newer servers that speak TCP/IP already are also available
cheaply. The DS300 speaks TCP/IP, but still needs MOP to load. The DS700 is
the latest-and-greatest standalone server and speaks TCP/IP, can boot with
BOOTP, and may have internal flash memory (in some models). The DS900 is the
DS700's big brother for use in the DEChub chassis. DS90's have a wide variety
of models - it started out as an ultra-low-end box, but customer dissatisfac-
tion caused new models to be introduced. At least one model runs software
comparable to the DS700, but unless you're sure which one it is, you might
get stuck with one of the turkeys.

  A "junk-grade" PC (386, 100MB IDE drive) could get a free BSD or Linux
loaded and work as a terminal server for you as well.

