Bruce Hoult wrote:
> 
> In article <7fnb76$gms$1@news.doit.wisc.edu>, "Andy Glew"
> <glew@cs.wisc.edu> wrote:
> 
> > It is unfortunate that there are not enough performance counters
> > on the P6 to measure bus utilization while you are also trying to
> > measure things like IPC. There are only 2 such counters.
> 
> Interesting that the PPC604 had only two counters, but the 604e and 750
> both increase this to four counters.
> 
> Are the PII performance counters identical?  i.e. can they both count
> exactly the same things, or different things?  The PPC ones let you
> monitor some things in any register, but each one has some unique things
> that only it can count (which is I guess done because they chose a
> particular number of bits to represent what to count and then found that
> they wanted to monitor more different things than that...)

The P5 & P6 counters have a lot of bits available to specify what to
count (16 bits/counter, with one or two bits taken to specify stuff like
cycles/instructions).

It will probably be a while before a cpu architect needs more than 8 or
16K of different countable hardware events.

What would have been really nice would be more counters, with the
current setup you really need a steady-state/repeatable environment to
get by the two-counter limit.

I speculated that Intel would increase this limit when I first revere
engineered the Pentium version of the counters back in 1994.
Unfortunately, so far I've been wrong. :-(

Terje

