Argh. I'll check the PSU manual this evening. The regulator
that's failed is the one that runs the memory. It's a separate 
plug-in module in the 2.5v PSU. Are you certain there's nothing
that could be shorting out the +5v memory rail? It might be worth
taking the 2.5v PSU out and examining the memory regulator board,
just to see what state it's in. Also, examine the cabling between the 
PSU and the CPU backplane for obvious signs of damage. 

I have had something vaguely similar happen: I managed to put
the Unibus cable to the expansion box into the wrong slot,
and when I powered up the machine, it short-circuited the 
main CPU +5v rail. One of the conductors in the cable blew
out, and the regulator fail LED for the main 5v rail came
on. However, the PSU had just shut down without being damaged,
and all that was needed was to repair the cable. It's just possible
that something similar has happened in your case. 

Sigh. I think you're probably having a harder time than most
getting this machine going. As far as the console problems are
concerned, I've never seen a 750 that emitted a series of '@'
characters on power-up as part of its normal behaviour, and
I've never seen a machine that *required* to load the console
cassette before being able to come up to the chevron prompt. 
That may just be because all the machines I've dealt with have
been of roughly the same vintage and therefore not new enough
to exhibit this behaviour. *However*, the fact that you've
been getting no sensible signals visible on the console port
makes me still think that this is down to a wiring problem. Though
if your memory power supply is shot, it's a moot question. 

I can't help wondering if these problems are related. 

James

