> Does anyone have any documentation/specs on these machines?  (Does anyone
> REMEMBER these things?)

From the System Spec:

     The LX3 memory system provides for up to 768 MBytes of DRAM, 32
kBytes
     of primary cache, and 512 kBytes of secondary cache in 15 ns SRAM.

     The LX3+ memory system provides for up to 1 GBytes of DRAM, 32 
kBytes
     of primary cache, and 1 MBytes of secondary cache in 15 ns SRAM.

     The integral graphics assist of the APECS chipset is not used.

     On LX3, six industry-standard, 72-pin SIMM sockets allow 60 or  70 
ns
     DRAM  memory to be added to the MLB.  Memory must be added as pairs
of
     SIMMs, to make up to three banks of two  SIMMS  each.   They  must 
be
     populated  in  adjacent  pairs  as the memory bus width is 66 bits
(64
     bits of data plus two bits of parity).  Both SIMMs in a pair  must 
be
     identical.

     On LX3+, eigth industry-standard, 72-pin SIMM sockets allow 60  or 
70
     ns DRAM memory to be added to the MLB.  Memory must be added in
groups
     of four SIMMs, to make one or two banks.  They must  be  populated 
in
     adjacent groups of four, as the memory bus width is 132 bits (128
bits
     of data plus four bits of parity).  All  SIMMs  in  a  group  must 
be
     identical.

     It is possible to use 33-bit SIMMs, instead  of  the  standard 
36-bit
     SIMMs,  since  the  LX3  and LX3+ use longword (not byte) parity. 
The
     unused pins in this case are 35, 37, and 38.

     Several SIMM sizes are supported allowing memory  configurations 
from
     16  Mbytes to 1 Gbyte.  If banks of different sizes are used
together,
     there is no restriction on physical ordering of the SIMM  pairs.  
The
     larger  banks  must  be  initialized  by  firmware to occupy the
lower
     addresses, since the base address of  each  bank  is  required  to 
be
     naturally aligned with its size.

     The SIMMs supported are of two kinds, ones having  a  single  bank 
of
     DRAMs  and  ones  having  two  banks.   The  SIMMs  with two banks
are
     configured by the firmware by setting the sub-bank enable bit  in 
the
     bank configuration register.

     EDO (Extended Data Out) SIMMs are supported.  If these SIMMs are
used,
     the page-mode cycle time can be reduced by one 15-ns memory
controller
     cycle.

     The number of address lines used varies according to the size  of 
the
     SIMM.

I have to add that this system tends to run its memories closer to the
edge of their specs, and that _many_ SIMMs we tested did not work
reliably.  The SIMMs sold by DIGITAL (or Compaq, if you will) for this
system do work.

Hope this helps,

