In article <y4n1zzg5om.fsf@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de>,
  Jan Vorbrueggen <jan@mailhost.neuroinformatik.ruhr-uni-bochum.de> writes:

>A big difference: Instruction caches work fine for SPEC-type applications, 
>but are almost useless for OLTP-type stuff. Lotsa loops in SPEC, little in
>OLTP. See posts about two years ago on this topic, citing a study by Bob
>Supnik (one of the Alpha architects).

Actually it was Dick Sites (one of the Alpha architects).

Applications have their performance determined by a mix of features
of a computer system.  For different applications there are different
features which are most important for determining performance.

Similarly, different benchmark suites have their performance determined
by a mix of features of a computer system.  The best benchmark for
determining performance of an application is to benchmark the application itself.
An alternative measurement is to use a benchmark suite which uses similar
system features as are used by the application.

SPECint depends (mostly but definitely not entirely) on the performance
	of the integer execution core and the first level cache. 

SPECfp depends (mostly but definitely not entirely) on the bandwidth of data
	fetch from main memory.  But the recent couple of generations of
	systems sometimes have large enough lower level caches such that
	the bandwidth of a suitably large cache determines performance.

OLTP benchmarks (TPC-x) depend (mostly but definitely not entirely) on the
	bandwidth of instruction fetch from main memeory (see the Dick Sites
	studies), the amount of parallelism available, and the amount of
	precomputation done.

Streams depends (mostly and almost entirely) on the data bandwidth of the memory
	system.  (On recent Alpha systems, how the compiler does prefetching
	is a very important component of memory bandwidth).



