> 
> And, in the future, what clock will the EV57 be shipping at? I don't
> know. You don't know. Yet you're posting to Usenet about it. Thank you
> for sharing.

  Well the title of the ISSCC98 paper on the shrunk 21164PC is "A 667 MHz
  RISC Microprocessor".  If you follow these kinds of things long enough
  you know that that means it will likely never exceed that speed in its
  0.28 um process in any volume and will be introduced at a significantly
  lower rate.

  It doesn't really matter if its 500, 600, or 800 MHz; the EV5 core
  is obsolete.  For many apps the EV5 at only 400 MHz spent more than
  half of its time doing sweet f**k all waiting for cache line fills.
  The frontside L2 doesn't leave much room to enhance the system interface
  performance either.

  So you are right Greg, I don't know what speed grades the 21164PC could
  or will be shipped at.  But the fact is it really doesn't matter, you
  could never run it fast enough in a low cost system and have it be
  competitive with mainstream high volume x86 parts it would face.  And
  yes, I will share this opinion too with Usenet, no thanks necessary.


All opinions strictly my own.
