  Hey pal what's your major malfunction?  Don't rip on the people
  pointing out the obvious.  I want Alpha to succeed commercially
  and become a high volume ISA as much as anyone and probably
  Mr. Song does too (it would certainly help his country's trade
  position in microprocessors  ;-)

  But trying to refight the last war with yesterday's weapons like
  the 21164PC is no recipe for success.  The monstrous high volume,
  leading edge process, x86 money machine has chewed up just about
  every other RISC pretender to the desktop and Alpha's only chance
  is that Compaq and Samsung start to execute a whole lot better
  than DEC did birthing the 21264.

  Bring out low cost EV6 and EV67 boards and systems.  Give away
  the damn compilers for now.  For god's sake do something about
  replacing the jurassic 8400 series. Set up ISV support and porting
  centers, pay them if you have to to get the hot technical apps onto
  Alpha.  Figure out why the lame ass Xeon is so competitive on TPC
  and incorporate the lessons learned ASAP.
  
  You can rant and rave all you want but unlike "true believers",
  actual customers need to perceive a real benefit to buying what
  is so far a niche product with relatively tiny installed base.
  The software they want to run has to be available on Alpha and
  they have to have a warm and fuzzy feeling that the architecture
  will persist and succeed in the future.

  Don't shoot the messenger just because "wishing really hard"
  isn't enough.

