In article <371e231d.0@news.destek.net>, cherkus@unimaster.com (Dave Cherkus) writes:
|> Andy Glew (glew@cs.wisc.edu) wrote:
|> > 
|> > At any particular time, there is only one page table en force, meaning
|> > that at ant time a process can directly access only 2^32 bytes, scattered
|> > over a 2^36 byte physical address space.
|> > 
|> 
|> Yes, this summarizes it well.
|> 
|> Can you discuss how the OS should use this?

Um, just as it was handled in the 60s? Essentially as a ramdisk with
block moves to/from chunks of RAM outside the kernel's `normal' 32-bit
address space?  See, e.g., the paper in Bell and Newell about ECS on
CDC machines.  Or 2.11BSD.   (Did I miss the rhetorical point?)


|> Another posting said most UNIX kernels presume they can access the
|> entire address space of one process at the same time they retain access
|> to the kernel's code and data.  So, if the size of the kernel is K, the
|> max size a process can be is 2^32-K.

In the late 80s there was one Unix port which used the x86 segment
prefixes to give all of the 2^32-bit address space to a user process.
See also the descriptions of 2.11BSD.

|> Are you expecting that presumption to change?

'Snot unduly difficult (albeit a bit slower; copyin/copyout and
syscall-copy-in have to use explicit segmentation prefixes).
Problem is, it doesn't really buy you much user address space.


|> Or do other operating systems have a better way to use this?
|> 
|> Or are you expecting the kernel to provide a programming interface
|> where the process can treat part of its 2^32-K address space as a
|> window into the 2^36 or greater address space?
|> 
|> Or are you presuming support for large processes isn't important,
|> and the extra address space should be used to keep more 2^32-K-sized
|> processes in memory at a time?

For a multiprocessor compute server running several batch-style
simulations, that can actually be fairly effective. Small segment,
though, and those users usually want FP bandwidth. so they may already
be looking elsewhere.



