In article <371e231d.0@news.destek.net>,
Dave Cherkus <cherkus@unimaster.com> wrote:
>> At any particular time, there is only one page table en force, meaning
>> that at ant time a process can directly access only 2^32 bytes, scattered
>> over a 2^36 byte physical address space.
>Can you discuss how the OS should use this?

Well, Andy mentioned one:  you can have a bunch of processes, each limited to
4Gbytes of virtual address space, ocuppying a total of 64Gbytes of physical
memory, without swapping any of them (or any *parts* of them) out.  So, in
other words, you can have, say, 24 processes, each of which requires about
3Gbytes of memory, all fully resident at the same time.

However, that's probably not the best use for it.

Another use for it, which I suspect is what it was intended for, is in large
MP systems, with lots of CPU's.  Consider, for example, a system with 1024
CPU's.  At that number of CPU's, you are very likely to want more than 4Gbytes
of physical memory; using extra address bits, available only to the operating
system, would allow you to exchange "messages" between nodes with very little
overhead.

The most common uses will be RAM disk (which is how NT is doing it, I
believe), and via special-purpose APIs to access it (Oracle does this for SCO
or Solaris/x86, I have been told).




