No, speed of use. A Torx fastener only needs a maximum of 1/10 of a
rotation for the bit to be seated (being five lobed, the bit starting
exactly out of phase with the fastner, moving the shortest path) where
a Phillips may need 1/8 rotation. The Torx also stay on the bit better,
so the manufacturing line worker doesn't drop as many, wasting time to
reload the tool. (They use air or electric driven expensive drivers.)


