>SPECint and SPECfp are composed of a particular kind of non-IO-bound
>workstation application. Thus, you should not expect any relevance to
>something like an OLTP appliction.

SPECfp especially, and also SPECint, are largely irrelevant to OLTP
performance.

But just in case it needs to be said: for the purposes of benchmarking,
OLTP benchmarks are nearly always CPU limited, not IO bound.
Mainly because you can keep buying more disk controllers, spreading
the workload over more spindles, until your CPUs become the limiter
(or at least until memory bus/interconnect bandwidth becomes the limiter).
This is slightly less true for TPC-D, although it is rapidly becoming more
true for TPC-D as query optimizers get better.

Saying that OLTP is IO bound is
a) realistic for some, but not all, customer configurations
but b) unrealistic for most benchmark configurations;
it nonetheless remains widely believed to be universally
true.






