> Greg Lindahl (lindahl@pbm.com) wrote:
> An interesting comp.arch question is why the 21264 platforms are not doing
> really well on transaction processing benchmarks. One would think the
> memory subsystem coupled with that execution engine would be kickin'
> butt.

The memory subsystem is good at throughput, but latency is an
equalizer.  If TPC is limited by main memory latency, all
well-designed machines will be equally fast on this benchmark.  BTW, a
Celeron-450 on a BX mainboard (100MHz bus clock) easily beats our
XP1000 on this (~130ns vs. ~200ns in lmbench).  On multiprocessor
machines the relations may be different, however.

Another reason could be latency for accesses in the L2 cache.  But
interestingly, the lmbench numbers for the XP1000 are on par here with
the Celeron-450 (~25ns), although it has 4MB off-chip cache and the
Celeron has 128K on-chip cache and is overclocked.  Has Intel slowed
down the Celeron's L2 cache artificially?

> We also have the recent discussion showing a 5x performance loss on branchy
> code with a 21264. I doubt even something as fast as a 21264 is going to do
> at all well if it takes a 5x hit for unaligned branches and the compilation
> system isn't making a good attempt to deal with that. (Not that I think
> this is true, but it always sucks to get some fast new box and find out
> your code is pathological...)

I guess you are referring to my threading microbenchmark here. The P6
also takes quite a hit for branch misprediction, too, but it took a
lot more work to get it to mispredict (alternating between two targets
did not do it, I had to alternate between three targets) than the
21264 (where the worst case occurs with the plain code).  However,
once this particular problem is known, a compiler can easily avoid it.

> (I am however looking at
> specifying a dual processor server for an integer only workload. So far I
> haven't seen any evidence that the DS20 boards are going to be much, if
> any, win over Xeons. Anyone got pointers to data saying otherwise?)

SPECint?  BTW, if you want to save money, why consider Xeons? Why not
plain Pentium-IIs (fixing Celerons probably isn't an option)?

