In message <FAKCxA.7Gx@kithrup.com> sef@kithrup.com (Sean Eric Fagan) writes:
>Another use for it, which I suspect is what it was intended for, is in large
>MP systems, with lots of CPU's.  Consider, for example, a system with 1024
>CPU's.  At that number of CPU's, you are very likely to want more than 4Gbytes
>of physical memory; using extra address bits, available only to the operating
>system, would allow you to exchange "messages" between nodes with very little
>overhead.

This segment register business began with the 8086, which used a segment
register to extend the addressable range to 20 bits of physical memory
(1 whole megabyte!).  The segment register scheme was also intended to
facilitate dynamic relocation of code and data blocks within the main memory,
which was a real problem for multi-tasking systems lacking virtual memory.

Later x86 generations simply carried along the segment registers
for compatibility.



