>I've seen this too.  And it's not trivial to tell the difference
>between the CPU being the limiter and the memory bus/interconnect
>bandwidth being the limiter.  I haven't seen machines that offer good
>statistics about bus utilization.  Most CPUs now have performance
>counters that let you deduce average cycles per instruction, cycles per
>load/store, cache misses, etc. which can indirectly give you a feel
>about bus utilization but it'd be nice if chipsets would give us some
>indication of average bus utilization for memory and io busses.


Intel's P6 family of processors (Pentium Pro, Pentium II, Pentium III,
Xeon, Celeron - basically all current Intel processors) all have
performance counter events for things like

+ cycles in which the data bus is busy
+ cycles in which the address bus is busy
+ bus transactions of various types

with flavours that count just the local processor's accesses,
and all accesses that the local processor snoops.

I believe there is also a straightforward

+ counter of bus clocks

so that you do not even need to know the CPU clock / bus clock
multiplier - you can determine it.


I made sure that there were distinct events for transactions
and cycles the bus was busy, because there is not always a
constant factor relationship since not all transactions use the
data bus for the same length of time.



Therefore, it is straightforward to measure bus utilization statistics.


It is unfortunate that there are not enough performance counters
on the P6 to measure bus utilization while you are also trying to
measure things like IPC. There are only 2 such counters.
But if you have a stable workload, you can make repeated runs.
I have also found it instructive to have a program periodically change
what is being measured: e.g. measure bus utilization over a millisecond,
then switch to IPC, then switch to cache misses, etc.
I hope that future processors will have more counters, so
better "snapshots" can be taken.


This does not help you with I/O bus activity, since the CPU does not
see such activity.  I believe, however, that Intel's north-bridge chipsets
provide similar counters for PCI bus activity.






