Eric Hildum wrote:
> 
> 
> The message values and associated text were built by an internal tool (SDL) and
> linked in as an additional object module - the same tool was used to build all
> the different header files (C, Pascal, etc.) used to support the different
> compiler environments.
> 


  Actually, error messages are defined/compiled using MESSAGE and are
available for users as well.  The message facility allows creation of
objects with the error message symbols, as well as creation of a object
used to create a shareable image which contains the text of the error -
so that when you do something like a SYS$EXIT(status) or
LIB$SIGNAL(status) -- you will get the error text displayed by the
system.

  SDL is a structure definition language that allows a language
independent definition of structures, and other symbols.  It allows the
output to be generated for most common languages.  It's a tool used
frequently on VMS to allow mixed language implementation without needed
to manage language specific definitions.



