In article <7fdlck$114a$1@news.doit.wisc.edu>, "Andy Glew" <glew@cs.wisc.edu> writes:
:
:>>: #if DEBUG
:>>:     #define FLAG_ERROR(error) if(error!=noErr) Debugger()
:>>: #else
:>>:     #define FLAG_ERROR(error)
:>>: #endif
:
:>
:>As I pointed out elsewhere, that's irrelevant when you are making calls
:>into the OS and problems are returned via error numbers. Unless, I guess,
:>you put a wrapper around every such call throwing an error, which is
:>basically identical to my scheme.


  OpenVMS tends to use either distributed error handling (where each 
  call uses explicit checks -- common on simpler applications), or it 
  uses centralized or module-level or subroutine-level handling using 
  OpenVMS signals and signal handlers (typical of larger and more
  complex applications).

:
:#define CATCH_ALL( errfunc )   try { errfunc } catch ( * ) { Debugger(); }

  or my typical use of:

  #include <stsdef.h>

  ...

  RetStat = ...
  if (!$VMS_STATUS_SUCCESS( RetStat ))
    lib$signal( SS$_DEBUG, AscicDebugCommand );

  RetStat = ...
  if (!$VMS_STATUS_SUCCESS( RetStat ))
    return RetStat;

  --


  More than most any sane person cares to know about this topic -- signals 
  and condition handling -- is included in the OpenVMS Programming Concepts 
  Manual... :-)


