I know the GS140 is the 8400 - i.e. basically an "old hat" (saturating 
the backplane even faster with the 21264 than the 21164). The problem is 
that Wildfire isn't shipping yet (it may ship in Q4). So it looks like 
the GS140 has to play Compaq Alpha top dog for Q2 and Q3, too.

> You should use the ES40 (recently shipping) for your
> comparison, since you're comparing to a future Xeon system. ]
I had a one-on-one with Jesse Lipcon (Compaq Alpha VP) a couple of
weeks ago. Expect no miracles for the ES40. Results for the quad machine 
are in line with the results from the DS20, maybe a little better. Hint: 
The DS20 is worse on TPC-C than a dual Xeon. 

So, if I were talking about the ES40, it might actually be more damaging.
The ES40 is the latest and greatest, the GS140 is not. But I'll keep my 
mouth shut until the ES40 benchmarks are officially out. That's why I was 
referring to the GS140. And yes, I know the DS20 is great on STREAMS and fp.

And by the way, if seen and touched the "future" Xeon system two months
ago (and pulled a CPU board out of it). I expect the benchmark results to 
show up at TPC R.S.N. 
 
> If I were in the x86 division, I'd be wondering when Merced will come
> out, and how badly the crunch of 32-bit memory limits is going to hurt
> me until it arrives. Ram is getting cheaper every day. But the x86
> folks can sleep assured that you are going to be banging their drum on
> comp.arch.
> 
Well, thanks for the flowers (as usual). Anyway, regarding me being
uncritical towards Intel, I think my position on the Merced is known...
Yes, of course the 32-bit limit is tough for Intel and NT, it's probably 
the biggest single obstacle NT needs to overcome for further performance
growth. This is no news. But it is *quite* some news that at this moment,
a n-way (n being 2, 4, 8) 21264 does NOT beat an n-way Xeon anymore on
TPC-C. If I were in the x86 division at Intel, I'd order double-cheese
double-ham with my next pizza for this one.

I have the PSE36 SDK and from a programmer's perspective, it is a terrible
hack compared to a flat memory model. However, it is probably acceptable 
if the issue is porting a few key packages, like Oracle8, SQL7 and the 
R/3 application servers. 

BTW, I never wrote the Alpha is dead blah blah. It just happens that
there are a few things that need to be done in the Alpha world right 
now, like cheap mass production, cheap K7/21264 motherboards , .25 and 
soon .18, and pieces in the 7-800 MHz range.

Cheers,
    HK

