young_r@eisner.decus.org (Rob Young) writes:

>"Wildfire servers will offer 16-way, 32-way, 64-way, and ultimately 120-way
>configurations based on an 800-MHz Alpha processor - the EV67 - as part of a
>concerted effort to compete directly with IBM, Sun, and Hewlett-Packard."

>	At a nice price too.  So high-end, yes.  Low-end also.  Watch
>	the other RISC vendors begin to squeel.  They are the ones that
>	won't be able to compete.


I'm sure the VMS customers will eat those up.  But it won't get Solaris
or HP-UX customers to switch, they can get systems up to 64 way (Sun
E10K) and 128 way (HP 4x NUMA V2500) today.  And by the time Wildfire is
out, Sun will be close to shipping the US 3, HP the PA-8600 and both
will have or be close to having next generation datacenter boxes out as
well.  Market share is important.  If Sun had introduced the SuperSparc
with a 6% market share instead of 50%, they would have been bankrupt by
1994.  Alpha has been at or near the top performance wise since intro,
but while it has maintained a rosy picture for VMS, it hasn't helped
DEC/Compaq's Unix business at all.  AFAIK they had a larger market share
for Ultrix in the late 80s than they have ever had with Unix on the
Alpha.  With Alpha they did a very good job of demonstrating how to
migrate a customer base with VMS, and how to alienate a customer base
with Ultrix.

-- 
