Peter da Silva <peter@baileynm.com> wrote:
>In article <7ffvbu$dv6$1@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>,
>Terry Murphy <tsmurphy@students.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>>In article <7ffqdc$4ch@news2.newsguy.com>,
>>Greg Lindahl <lindahl@pbm.com> wrote:
>>>If I were in the x86 division, I'd be wondering when Merced will come
>>>out, and how badly the crunch of 32-bit memory limits is going to hurt
>>>me until it arrives. Ram is getting cheaper every day. But the x86
>>>folks can sleep assured that you are going to be banging their drum on
>>>comp.arch.
>
>>But Xeon supports 36 bit memory addressing
>
>Xeon supports multiple 32 bit segments, like every x86 processor since, IIRC,
>the 386. It has 36 bits of external address lines, but the programming model
>is still 32 bit segments and nobody's ever done anything useful with that over
>the past, what, 15 years...

Well, it's not _quite_ just "32 bit segments".  Xeon (and all PPro-derived
cores) have the "giant page" extensions from the i860 MMU (which was 
otherwise basically a 486 MMU) grafted on.  The P5 has _some_ of them.

Unfortunately, it's a little hard to use those for anything except maybe
reducing the PTE overhead of a frame buffer or other externally mapped
region, but they are there.

