lindahl@pbm.com (Greg Lindahl) writes:

>dsiebert@icaen.uiowa.edu (Doug Siebert) writes:

>[ On the low end... ]
>> In integer performance,
>> the 21264 is not far enough ahead of the Pentium III (and probably only
>> twice what a $100 Celeron can do)

>You'll have to fill us in as to what application you had in mind;
>Spec95 int tells a quite different story. But the 21264 isn't even
>what the announced strategy is for the low-end; the 21164 at high
>clocks is the next move in that area.


Intel has a result for a system with a 400MHz Celeron (PPGA model $102)
and running at standard speed, no overclocking) for SPECint95 of 14.9.
The highest SPECint for the 21264 I see is 30.3, on a big server class
machine with huge cache (it is less than 28 for the "normal" desktop
system)  Dell sells a Pentium III Xeon system with SPECint 22.1, within
spitting distance of the 27.7 for the DS20.  So where was what I said
wrong?  If you are talking 21164 the picture is no better, you'd need a
1GHz+ 21164 by my calculations to beat the 21264 numbers, and I suspect
$100 will not be the price.


>> On the higher end, their Unix has a miniscule market share compared to
>> Solaris and HP-UX, and that is unlikely to change.

>I know that FUDding everything is very popular today, but it's sales
>that make money, not market share.


Yes, but the market share is the proof you are making the sales, and like
it or not, people base purchasing decisions on market share.  Its easier
to get management to decide to go with Sun or HP on your big ERP
implementation than Compaq Alpha for precisely that reason.  If your
bolt on and plug in choices for SAP are restricted you make an already
difficult process even harder, as an example.  Obviously if you are an
independant research type you can buy highest performance you can afford
and not worry if next time you buy it is something completely different,
but that's a small segment of the market.

