We have a customer with a frame relay WAN using routeabout Access EW
units; the config is a star, with a 512K frame connection at the central
site supporting 10 PVCs to the remote offices which each have 56K 
connections.  Due to the requirement to run LAT, the network is currently
100% transparent bridge, with netbios filtering enabled to reduce the
rather startling amount of caca the pcs try to pump out as broadcasts.

The routers do not have IP configured; they do have IP host access con-
figured to allow telnet-ing in to them for management.  Additional 
bridge protocol filtering is turned on for the LAVC protocol, Appletalk,
IPX, etc, but _not_ IP.

If the remote LAN is disconnected from the remote router (cutting all
connection to the central site and the rest of the WAN), its PCs come up
just fine.  If they are booted while the WAN is connected, every one of
them complains that their address is already in use.  When we check from
the central site (and that covers the whole rest of the WAN) there is no
indication that those addresses exist anywhere on the network; we have
some basic sniffing tools, plus ping can't see them.  Reconnect the remote
LAN to the WAN and the PCs complain again.  Further testing below was done
by: 1 - disconnect the remote LAN, 2 - reboot the PCs, 3 - restart the
remote router, 4 - reconnect the remote LAN to the remote router, and 5 -
start testing with pings, etc.

The central office just got an internet connection, and has decided to 
use the Alphacluster there as the pop-3 server for the PCs, and the
SMTP transfer point for outbound mail.  Works great for all the local
PCs, but PCs at the initial remote site have a problem:



After the router at that remote site reboots, the cluster can ping 
individual remote PCs (and they can ping the cluster).  The router
can ping the cluster, and can ping _some_ of the PCs at the remote
site (which can all ping eachother), but after 5-10 minutes something
happens.  The cluster loses the ability to ping any remote PC, the
PC loses access to the cluster, the router can still see the cluster
(via its WAN connection to the central router) but will ping the PCs
and get one (and only one) ping back, then nothing.  Any further
attempts to ping the PCs fail completely.  Restart the remote router 
and the sequence starts over.

The customer is using 'private' addressing with 32 consecutive class C
address ranges (mask 255.255.255.224 for now, so its all treated as a
single network).  Each site gets its addresses assigned out of the
Class C range that will be its own private range down the road when 
full IP routing replaces the current bridged setup.  All are using the
.224 mask now to treat the 32 * class C range as a single network.  The
firewall in use provides NAT for internet access, though we believe this
is totally unrelated to the problems we're seeing.

Any idea what is going on?  Some kind of bridging wierdness?  Bridge
database learns something wonky and stops forwarding?  LAN Manager and
NetBEUI appear to keep working fine, as does LAT from the PCs...

My apologies if this message format is messed up; st00pid terminal 
emulator and VI on my ISP just don't get along.

Any ideas or info greatly appreciated.

Rich Jordan
rjordan@mcs.net




