Okay, I had a look at the printset last night. 
F CON FST is a TTL-level signal that, when grounded,
has the effect of forcing the console to run at it highest
speed (38400, believe it or not!) regardless of the settings
of the backplane jumpers. It isn't normally used. I would
leave it disconnected at the 750 end of the cable. (If you're
interested, it emerges in the L0004 circuit diagrams, on the
same page as the UARTS for the console and TU58 serial lines,
as FORCE CON FAST). 

Can you do some basic checks for me?
1) When the machine is powered up, is the Power OK
LED on the back of the power controller lit, with none
of the other LEDs lit?
2) Are the PSU rails within margins? (check the printset
for the actual values -- I can find test-points for you
if you want). 
3) Use the lamp-test button on the bottom edge of the front
panel PCB to check that all the front-panel indicators are
working (feel along the bottom edge of the board with the 
front door open to find it).
4) For an RDM-equipped 750, this is what should happen when
you power up with the power-on action switch in the Halt
position:
front-panel Power LED comes on; RD FAULT indicator lights
for a few seconds and then goes out; there is then a brief
pause while the memory controller initialises memory; then
the Run LED should give a brief flash. The 'Error' LED 
should be glowing softly all the time, indicating that
the CPU clock is running. It would be useful to know what 
your machine does on power-up.
5) Does anything at all happen when you hit the front-panel
reset button, with the keyswitch in 'Local'?

James



