Greg Lindahl wrote:
> 
> dsiebert@icaen.uiowa.edu (Doug Siebert) writes:
> 
> [ On the low end... ]
> > In integer performance,
> > the 21264 is not far enough ahead of the Pentium III (and probably only
> > twice what a $100 Celeron can do)
> 
> You'll have to fill us in as to what application you had in mind;
> Spec95 int tells a quite different story. But the 21264 isn't even
> what the announced strategy is for the low-end; the 21164 at high
> clocks is the next move in that area.
> 

But SPECint isn't really an application and neither is SPECfp. This 
has always been the Alpha problem. 

Great SPECint95 and SPECfp95 numbers and not much else. For 
example in the NT market NT on Alpha has never bested NT/Intel
on any of the public benchmarks like TPC-C, TPC-D, NotesBench
SPECweb, SPECsfs and this is despite having much better CPU 
performance, somewhat better I/O and memory subsystems and 
access to more memory.

These benchmarks cover the commercial workgroup server market.
The high volume good margin market that CPQ need to make 
inroads into with Alpha.

Even the current DS20 fares badly when compared with a quad 
XEON machine using OLTP. 

At the high end of the NT space Digitals problems are emplified 
by the 8400 5/625 running NT having a lower SAP/SD number than an 8 
CPU pentium pro box. 

In the UNIX market the same story continues with SUN, HP and 
even Sequent leading CPQ on most of the major benchmarks. 

TPC-C, TPC-D, SAP/SD, BaaN BRU, NotesBench, SPECweb, SPECsfs
there isn't a single major server benchmark where Digital/CPQ
has a leading number. Now this could be construed a marketing 
systems using benchmarks but consistency of Digital/CPQ's 
ability not to get good numbers is pretty compelling.

Finally in the space where you might expect Digital to do 
well, namely HPC there are virtually no CPQ/Digital boxes 
in the top500 list, which is made up of predominantly SGI,
Sun and IBM boxes with a couple of large Intel systems.

The reason for this is fairly obvious. The 8400/GS140 is 
a really old design and needs replacing. NT and DU application 
performance on Alpha seems not to reflect the Alpha's 
SPECint/fp perfromance suggesting that Digital/CPQ need 
to spend more on application tuning. ISV's are confused 
about which platform they should support resulting in some 
important defections from both DU and NT/Alpha like 
UG.
 
> > On the higher end, their Unix has a miniscule market share compared to
> > Solaris and HP-UX, and that is unlikely to change.
> 
> I know that FUDding everything is very popular today, but it's sales
> that make money, not market share.
> 
> > Their overall profit numbers may look better in the long run pursuing a
> > VMS only strategy for Alpha, even when that makes the Alpha chips more
