Dave Cherkus wrote:
> 
> Andy Glew (glew@cs.wisc.edu) wrote:
> >
> > At any particular time, there is only one page table en force, meaning
> > that at ant time a process can directly access only 2^32 bytes, scattered
> > over a 2^36 byte physical address space.
> >
> 
> Yes, this summarizes it well.
> 
> Can you discuss how the OS should use this?
> 
> Another posting said most UNIX kernels presume they can access the
> entire address space of one process at the same time they retain access
> to the kernel's code and data.  So, if the size of the kernel is K, the
> max size a process can be is 2^32-K.
> 
> Are you expecting that presumption to change?
> 
> Or do other operating systems have a better way to use this?

This isn't a radically new situation; later PDP-11's with MMU
have a 2**16 byte "virtual" address space and a 2**22 byte
"physical" address space.  And many of today's OS's (including
Unix, VMS, and Windows NT) can trace their heritage back to PDP-11's.
All but the earliest PDP-11 Unices do quite nicely when not all
of the kernel is directly accessible (many of them deal quite
nicely with overlays in the OS, as a matter of fact) so I would
postulate that the "2**32-K" limit is not so fundamental.

> Or are you expecting the kernel to provide a programming interface 
> where the process can treat part of its 2^32-K address space as a
> window into the 2^36 or greater address space?

This is a service provided by RT-11, RSX-11, RSTS/E, and some
other PDP-11 OS's.  It isn't conveniently done by user-mode
code under the PDP-11 Unices.  Support for large arrays through mapping
is built into high-level languages, such as Fortran, for these OS's.
And the linker/loaders for the OS's were quite aware of the
ability to do "virtual memory" overlays instead of "disk" overlays.

> Or are you presuming support for large processes isn't important,
> and the extra address space should be used to keep more 2^32-K-sized
> processes in memory at a time?

At least on the PDP-11 OS's support for both large processes and
for multiple processes are considered important.

You probably think of the days of physical address space being
larger than virtual address space as being "the bad old days", but
some of us still exercise our CRAW's daily :-).

