On VMS the error number returned in a 32 bit value was actually a bit structure.
three bits were allocated for the type of "error" with a very specific
definition. Another group of bits was allocated to indicate the facility
originating the error (standard library code, IO system, etc.) and the remainder
the actual error number itself. I always found the discipline of correctly
classifying the return values helpful in designing the code; they were:

Success - the operation had a correct result.
Informational - the operation had a correct result, and the message indicates
additional information.
Warning - the operation had a correct result, but a correctable error occured
during the operation
Error - an error occured during the operation. The operation completed, but the
results may not be correct.
Fatal Error - an error occured during the operation, and continued execution is
not possible.

The message values and associated text were built by an internal tool (SDL) and
linked in as an additional object module - the same tool was used to build all
the different header files (C, Pascal, etc.) used to support the different
compiler environments.

For some reason this does not seem to be a popular way of classifying errors;
the UNIX and Windoes style of everything is fatal and the OS will simply give up
with minimal or no useful information in case of an exception seems to be most
popular. This is the reason, I think, that most UNIX and Windows code (and code
developed by people initial trained on UNIX or Windows) is so delicate and bug
prone.

