Former Compaq CEO Rod Canion got the boot about eight years ago
for being too friendly with MIPS and the ACE consortium.  Perhaps
Intel and the Compaq internal x86 mafia had a lot of influence on
the board of directors.

Fast forward to the present, now Pfieffer is forced to walk the
plank.  He seemed to have demonstrated a firm support for Alpha, especially at
the very high end at the expense of Merced and IA-64.
Perhaps we have the same set of finger prints on the knives in
the back of these two CEOs.

Is this the portend of an increasing bitter internal struggle at
Compaq over which 64 bit architecture will dominate? Perhaps with
the partial settlement with the FTC, Intel feels more free to
advance its own interests.  Of course the FTC has its own concern
in the outcome of the 64 bit struggle at Compaq, as it has made
the ongoing support of Alpha a precondition of the takeover of
DEC.  Interesting times...


All opinions strictly my own.
