This is a good question.

Paul DeMone wrote:
> Fast forward to the present, now Pfieffer is forced to walk the
> plank.  He seemed to have demonstrated a firm support for Alpha, especially at
> the very high end at the expense of Merced and IA-64.
> Perhaps we have the same set of finger prints on the knives in
> the back of these two CEOs.
I think it's more prosaic issue: Money. If you loose money, you loose your
job. (In this case, it were lower profits, not actual losses).

> Is this the portend of an increasing bitter internal struggle at
> Compaq over which 64 bit architecture will dominate? 
Probably. And it's not only a question about 64bits, but also about 
which server to push to customers. The problem is like this:

1) AlphaServerGS140       37540 tpmC    79,43 $/tpmC     8x21264 575MHz
2) ProLiant XXX           35000 tpmC    20-25$/tpmC      8xXeon 500MHz

2) is just my estimate, but the real data for 2) should be released any 
day now. The picture is consistent for the DS20 (2 CPUs) and the upcoming 
4 CPU machine.

"Two hearts are beating in Compaq's bosom", so to speak. As long as the
Alpha had a clear performance advantage in systems, it had it's lease of
life. But as the Wildfire was delayed so much, and the 21264 seems to have
some problems realising its potential, that performance advantage is not 
what it used to be (except for some I/O and 64bit stuff, of course).

So, if I were in the x86 division, I'd ask the Alpha division some hard
questions. A factor of three in $/tpmC is just a bit too painful.

Cheers,
    HK

