> This debacle cannot be completely, or even largely, attributed to the
> acquisition of Tandem and DEC. Logistics, channels and excessive overhead
> have a lot to do with it.

Digital's channel system was a mess before it was added to Compaq's.  Adding
it to Compaq's only helped make the problem worse.

In the end Compaq will have to face the fact that in the under $2000
workstation market the only viable model will be direct customer ordering
that drives a build to order system.  The sub-$500 PC market has changed
this forever.  Prior to 18 months ago the name of the game was the best PC
one could sell for $2000.  This is because  prior to the Pentium II most
PC's sold were still underpowered and as a result folks would buy whatever
they could up to the magic $2K price point.  Once we started into the
generation of 300mHz 64mb users could now drop the traditional $2K price
point because for many of them anything faster than 300mHz 64mb systems
exceeded the need.  It was that shift from the best PC for $2K to the
cheapest 300mHz PC that  has obsoleted Compaq's channel model.

IMO that channel model should die the death it deserves.  Artificially
building profit into a system for channels partners by protecting the direct
sales price is something that is done for the benefit of the channel's
partners and not the customer.  If the channel partners truly add something
that the customer values than the customer will be willing to pay the
channel partner a higher price over direct sales.  Compaq's "Customer
Choice" was a case of Orwellian 1984 speak because there was no choice given
in price.  True customer choice would have been being able to decide if the
value add of the channel partner was worth a higher price...






