Terry C Shannon <shannon@world.std.com> writes:

>I do write a newsletter, but I don't line my walls with tinfoil, so I
>guess I'm not a true Conspiracy Theorist. I can think of a number of
>reasons why the Q will not scuttle Alpha at this point.

>o Alpha was not the proximate cause of the debacle.

>o Microsoft wants Win64 out the door and on a real hardware platform
>sooner rather than later.

>o As goes Alpha, so goes the entire VMS base.


I don't think anyone is seriously suggesting that Compaq would kill
Alpha.  But there are a lot of lines in between killing it and putting
your full marketing muscle/support/dollars behind it.

If you look at the Alpha as two markets -- higher end higher cost systems
running VMS and Tru64, and lower end lower cost workstation level systems
running NT, Linux and Tru64, its obvious where the money is going to be
made.  In the low end market they compete against x86 systems, which they
cannot compete against except for the few and far between customers who
care enough about FP to pay (much) more for it.  In integer performance,
the 21264 is not far enough ahead of the Pentium III (and probably only
twice what a $100 Celeron can do)  And you have to put up with less
software availability whether you choose NT, Alpha Linux, or Tru64.

On the higher end, their Unix has a miniscule market share compared to
Solaris and HP-UX, and that is unlikely to change.  But in VMS land,
they have a cash cow of satisfied customers will who keep coming back
over and over again.  Its easier to sell them expensive systems if you
don't sell a similar system for 10% of the cost as a cheap SMP NT box!
Their overall profit numbers may look better in the long run pursuing a
VMS only strategy for Alpha, even when that makes the Alpha chips more
expensive on a per chip basis.

I think DEC/Compaq's biggest problem with the Alpha has been AMD's
success in getting x86 price points down so low.  Of course, this has
been a problem for AMD too -- and everyone else except Microsoft, who
sells more fixed cost Windows licenses when low PC prices increase the
number of PCs shipped.  Gosh, sure sounds like the textbook definition
of a monopoly I learned in school!

-- 
