In article <3716568f.7348776@news.primenet.com>, Scott_Boston@ensyte.com (Le Beaux) writes:

>However, when I try 
>#tar tvf /dev/rmt0h
>
>tar: [offset 0]: I/O error
>
>Any guess on what this error could be from?

Some versions of tar attempt to guess the transfer size used to
write a tape by reading the first record using a very large
transfer size (1 MB or there-abouts).  I think it does something
similar writing.  But, not all tape drives support such a large
transfer size.  The I/O error could be cause by it trying to
use a larger tranfer size than the drive support, or that the
driver *believes* the drive supports.

The maximum transfer size for a drive is a parameter in /etc/ddr.dbase.
The default for unrecognized drives may be 64 KB.

You can check by looking at the error log to see what the error
was.  Prefer to use DECevent to format the error log (the dia
command, if DECevent is installed).  If you use uerf(8), use
the option "-o full" to get the full error listing.

>Suggestions?  Solutions?

First, don't write to tape when you're trying to extract from it...

Second, use an explicit transfer size on the tar command.  From
my experience, that will prevent it from trying to use a large
size.

>Scott Boston
>

