Newsgroups: comp.lang.c,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.dec

# uname -a
OSF1 pvtest.cc.iastate.edu V4.0 878 alpha

# cat ct.c
#include <stdio.h>

int foo[(3,4)];
int bar[3,4];

main(){ printf("sizes of foo and bar are %d, %d\n", sizeof(foo), sizeof(bar)); }

# cc  -g -O0 ct.c  -o ct
cc: Error: ct.c, line 4: Missing "]".
int bar[3,4];
---------^
cc: Error: ct.c, line 3: In the declaration of "foo", "3,4" is not constant, but occurs in a context that requires a constant expression.
int foo[(3,4)];
---------^

It seems to me that the first error message is clearly not helpful,
but what haven't been able to discover is:

   Is the result of:

          constant-expression comma-operator constant-expression

   still a constant-expression or not?  If not, why not (and it
   seems to me this is the only operator with this property?)

John
PS, FWIW, gcc accepts both of these and produces the 'expected' results
