John Hascall wrote in <7f567v$sp1$1@news.iastate.edu>:
> # uname -a
> OSF1 pvtest.cc.iastate.edu V4.0 878 alpha
> 
> # cat ct.c
> #include <stdio.h>
> 
> int foo[(3,4)];
> int bar[3,4];
> 
> main(){ printf("sizes of foo and bar are %d, %d\n", sizeof(foo), sizeof(bar)); }
> 
> # cc  -g -O0 ct.c  -o ct
> cc: Error: ct.c, line 4: Missing "]".
> int bar[3,4];
> ---------^
> cc: Error: ct.c, line 3: In the declaration of "foo", "3,4" is not constant, but occurs in a context that requires a constant expression.
> int foo[(3,4)];
> ---------^
> 
> It seems to me that the first error message is clearly not helpful,
> but what haven't been able to discover is:
> 
>    Is the result of:
> 
>           constant-expression comma-operator constant-expression
> 
>    still a constant-expression or not?  If not, why not (and it
>    seems to me this is the only operator with this property?)
> 
> John
> PS, FWIW, gcc accepts both of these and produces the 'expected' results

I just checked a draft of C9X (I don't have the current ANSI/ISO standard),
and it appears to me that the compiler is right:

"[ ]" contains an assignment-expression, not a general expression
(section 6.5.5: Declarators).  Therefor "int bar[3],..." is valid,
but "int bar[3,..." not, and it's not unreasonable for the compiler
to complain about the missing "]" that would enable it to continue
parsing.

And:
Constant expressions shall not contain comma operators (section 6.4:
Constant expressions).

(C9X allows variable-size arrays though, so "int foo[(3,4)]" should
then be ok.)

gcc has a different handling of "," and IMHO it violates the standard
in that respect.  Even gcc -ansi -pedantic doesn't emit a diagnostic
for this error (it violates the constraint in 6.3.16.1: Simple assignment,
referred to by 6.5.8: Initialization):

  char weekday[7] = { "Mon", "Tue", "Wed", "Thu", "Fri", "Sat", "Sun" };

(That's an actual example of something a friend who is just learning C
stumbled over.)

joerch



