> crw-rw-rw-   1 root     system     9,5120 Apr 14 16:37 /dev/rmt0l
> crw-rw-rw-   1 root     system     9,5124 Apr 14 16:37 /dev/rmt0m

So you get a directory listing of devices in the /dev directory.

> 
> Next I tried,
> 
> #cd
> #tar cvf /dev/rmt0h bin
> 
> and I get
> 
> a bin
> a bin/setx 1 Blocks

As another poster mentioned, you just overwrite the tape (the "c"
option creates a tar file on the device (or disk file) specified
after the "f" option. I do hope you have a copy of the original
tape...

> 
> However, when I try
> #tar tvf /dev/rmt0h
> 
> tar: [offset 0]: I/O error
> 
> Any guess on what this error could be from?  Suggestions?  Solutions?
> Anything?
>
Again another poster explained - you were trying to read tape after
the EOF.

Next time, you should:

1) Write protect any tape - at least then you can't overwrite your
   data.
2) Use tar tvf /dev/rmt0h to check the contents of the tape.
3) Use tar xvf /dev/rmt0h to copy the contents of the tape to the
   local directory (make sure this is where you want it to go).
 
