Uh, *why*?

The Linux vax and DECstation ports vaguely remind me of the Linux "port" to
the Apple Newton -- they made the bootloader run and declared that they had
"ported Linux", at a point in time when the thing didn't even run on the ARM
processors at all.

NetBSD will run on your DECstation, with a minimum of fuss, now -- just as
it has for many, many years.  If you can get a SCSI disk image, with
bootblocks, onto a CD-ROM, it'll probably boot from that -- jonathan? --
so long as you have a 512-byte-block CD-ROM drive.  Otherwise you can netboot
or, IIRC, boot from floppies.  NetBSD will happily run from a SCSI disk, too,
which I seem to distinctly recall "the Linux port to the DECstation" (a.k.a
the basically dead "Linux port to ARC MIPS" with a lot of s/ARC/DECstation/g
done on it) will not.

