TTK Ciar (ttk@for_mail_remove_this_and_fruit.larva.apple.shinma.org) wrote:
:   The languages I know with exception handling (C++, Java) have been 
: designed sufficiently poorly that lots of people still don't want to 
: use them.  

Probably the biggest barrier to use of C++ exception handling is lack of a
good binary compatible calling standard that supports it on any one
platform. (Much less having that level of support on many platforms so
exceptionful code would be portable.) I know of no platform where the OS
libraries could throw C++ exceptions and have something reasonable happen
in all (or even most) of the languages the platform supports. Error code
returns do not have this problem.  Microsoft's structured exception
handling was an attempt in that direction but last I looked it basically
wasn't compatible with either COM or their C++ implementation.

Point being that its pretty hard to let exceptions propagate across large
API lines right now. Still, using them internally works out pretty well.

-Z-



