Does anyone know how DU (or should I call it True64 ?) assign those rz
numbers for SCSI disks? (them man pages for rz did not help.)

Our problem is namely this:

We had a machine happily running Digital^H^H^H^H^H^H^H Compaq UNIX 4.0d
with the root disk numbered rz16. Yesterday we when we had to replace the
machine we simply transferred the disk to the new machine and LO AND
BEHOLD! rz16 disappeared! In its place the new machine asked for rz0 and
refused to boot as there was no rz0 in /dev. I managed to boot under
single-user mode but since the disk was mounted as read-only, I could not
modify my fstab or make new nodes.

Both machines have essentially the same configuration except that
1. the SCSI controllers are in different PCI slots, and
2. the old machine had 2 SCSI chains while the new one has only one.

I finally solved my problem and got back my rz16 by moving my cards and
cables around but as I am expecting this problem to appear rather often at
my site (don't ask me why, but we seem to be moving things around quite a
fair bit), I would like to know: 

1. how those rz numbers are assigned (random or based on some
   logical mapping?)
2. if I am unable to get the original rz numbers back, which files do I
   have to modify besides fstab ?
3. or is there an elegant way to change/swap system disks?

Thanks.

Stephen Wong
Department of Computational Science
National Univeristy of Singapore

ps. for this I prefer IRIX or Linux where devices are named based on their
    SCSI id rather than these cryptic rz numbers.



