In article <7f1ifd$89k$1@nuscc.nus.edu.sg>,
Wong Yeow Whye <cscwyw@leonis.nus.edu.sg> wrote:
>Does anyone know how DU (or should I call it True64 ?) assign those rz

8 * B + T

	B == SCSI bus number (scsi0, scsi1, etc)
	T == SCSI target address.

Yes, this does mean that if you have an ultra-wide controller you still
can't address more than 7 devices. I believe the fix (in DU 4.0something)
involves giving a controller two bus numbers.

>Both machines have essentially the same configuration except that
>1. the SCSI controllers are in different PCI slots, and
>2. the old machine had 2 SCSI chains while the new one has only one.

Sounds like it had 3 SCSI chains. If it really only had two I'd like to
see the boot messages and /usr/sys/conf/whatever config file (email).

>2. if I am unable to get the original rz numbers back, which files do I
>   have to modify besides fstab ?

None.

>3. or is there an elegant way to change/swap system disks?

You build the fstab and device entries on the old system before moving it.

