> In which case you need an "error adapter" function to deal with this.

My favorite is something like:
#ifdef CHECK_ERR
#define EXPECT_ZERO(f) {if (!(f)) <punt or enter debugger>}
#else
#define EXPECT_ZERO(f) {(void) f}
#endif

The #defines for the "errno setting procedures" are analogous.

I don't see a huge benefit to an exception model here, and any benefit
seems completely swamped by the costs.  (The arguments over C++, Ada,
Java "prove" that it isn't simple and since it is overhead....)  How
often is the response something other than punt or enter debugger and
how much should I pay for those exceptional cases?

BTW - Any "solution" which requires full source or adherence to "the
one true way" by other people isn't.

