>Am I the only person in the world who writes every single damn function he
>types up as 
>
>#if DEBUG
>    #define FLAG_ERROR(error) if(error!=noErr) Debugger()
>#else
>    #define FLAG_ERROR(error) 
>#endif
>
>    error=CallSomeFunc(...);
>    FLAG_ERROR(error);
>
>I have never seen any other code that does this, yet the pain it saves is
>immeasurable. Regardless of whether or not I can do anything useful
>subsequently with the error, the very fact that I catch the problem in the
>debugger at the source, not 50 corrupted memory blocks later is
>extraordinarily useful.

  Sometimes I'll do this (or some subset or variation on it):

#define DB_FOO  1
#define DB_BAR  2
#define DB_BAZ  4

static int  ErrResult = 0;
static long Iteratung = 0;
#define DEBUG ( DB_FOO | DB_BAZ )  
#define DB(X) if ( DEBUG & X ) 
#define DBWRAP(F)                  \
  if ( ( Iteratung++ | 1 ) &&      \
       ( ErrResult = F ) < 0 )     \
     if ( DEBUG ) Dump ( Iteratung, ErrResult )

int some_function ( key ) int key; { ... }

int baz_function ( p ) char *p;
  {
    DB(DB_BAZ) if ( p == NULL ) return -1;
    DB(DB_BAZ) if ( p[0] == 0 ) 
      fprintf ( stderr, "baz_function: passed zero-length arg\n" );
    ...
  }

int some_other_function ( blech ) struct blecht *blech; 
  {  
  int key;
  DBWRAP ( key = baz_function ( blech->str ) );
  DBWRAP ( some_function ( key ) );
  ...
  }

  And then I'll juggle DEBUG's definition depending on which part 
of the code I'm interested in debugging at the time.  When it's the 
sort of application gdb can handle elegantly, though, I'll often 
eschew with this kind of annotation and just use gdb.

  Having better debugging support in the language for making this 
sort of thing easier would be good, but I've not liked any such 
implementation I've seen.  I've been using gcc with the bounds-
checking mod lately; it does a good job at catching some errors 
at run-time and dumping debugging information without my having 
to explicitly code for it at all.

  -- TTK




