after the other, the others stay on).  But, no console output to the video
card.  No beeps, nothing!  Pulling the RAM causes the 1-3-3 pattern of beeps
(2 MB of RAM not found), so I know the machine is at least somewhat living. 
What in the world is wrong?

Long version: I'm having a bizarre problem with my AlphaStation 200 4/166. 
Yes, it's one of those OnSale bargains, and until recently worked quite well.
 I had been running it with the 24 MB of RAM that came with the system, and
decided to add an additional 64 MB of RAM.  I mean, I like this machine!

So, I order 2 32-MB true parity SIMMs 60ns.  I put them in the 2 vacant SIMM
slots, and boot the computer.  Voila!  88 MB of RAM!  Except for some reason,
NT will no longer boot (it panics right at the login screen).  Linux seems to
boot ok.  By the way, this is with the ARC firmware.

Ok, so not willing to leave well-enough alone, I figure that maybe the SIMMs
need to be reseated.  So, I open the machine up and reseat the SIMMS.  Now,
the machine won't even go to a firmware console.  No matter which SIMMs are
in, the machine does nothing.  Pulling the RAM causes the 1-3-3 beep pattern,
which signifies "no RAM found by SROM".  With RAM in, the machine powers on,
the drives reset, and the status lights on the back sit in a pattern of "all
lights except the last 3 on; the last 3 cycle on and off in a marquee
pattern".

What in the world is wrong?  I've tried various video cards (both general S3
PCI and DEC's TGA).  Even the old RAM doesn't work anymore.

If anyone has any suggestions, I'd love to hear them.  Feel free to e-mail
your suggestions to me at medavid@ix.netcom.com if you don't want to post
here.  I'm getting a bit frantic, since I've got some Linux/Alpha work I want
to do.

