No, I mean it does work on a PC. I suppose I should have said, "I hooked it up to 
an IBM compatible PC and the Adaptec BIOS detected it."

>: I've checked, it doesn't 
>:conflict with my hard drive SCSI ID. Do I need to do anything special 
>:before boot-up to get it recognized?
>
>  Nope.  Assuming there are no SCSI unit ID collisions -- and unlike a
>  Microsoft Windows or Windows NT system -- OpenVMS will automatically 
>  detect the appearance of a supported disk device and will then
>  autoconfigure the necessary device drivers.
>
>:                                     Also, does anyone have docs for 
>:this drive? I'm not doing well trying to guess what the DIP switches do.
>
>  Switches 1, 2, and 3 set the SCSI ID number.  For example,
>  SCSI ID is set as Up Down Down.  The position of the fourth 
>  switch has no effect -- the default setting is Down.
>
>    ID  1     2     3     4
>    --------------------------
>    0   Down  Down  Down  Down
>    1   Down  Down  Up    Down
>    2   Down  Up    Down  Down
>    3   Down  Up    Up    Down
>    4   Up    Down  Down  Down
>    5   Up    Down  Up    Down
>    6   Up    Up    Down  Down
>    7   Up    Up    Up    Down
>
>  If you want further help, we'll need to know the operating system,
>  version, general information about the SCSI bus, and an idea of
>  what else is connected to the bus.  Information specific to this
>  device from the console displays, as well as any self-test errors
>  reported, would also be useful.

Actually, the 4th DIP switch does seem to do the trick. It looks bizarre, however.
In addition to ESA0 and DKA300, I see several unnamed disks at ID 4 on LUN 0-7!
Of course, they're all my RRD40. I shrugged and typed "boot dka400" at the
console and it booted, very slowly, off my VMS install CD. I suppose there may be
something wrong with the unit, but I'll use it as long as it functions reliably.



