We came across a problem deep in the internals of QuickTime that only
occurred on one particular box. Sleuthing revealed the problem to be an
IDE controller that was insisting on a little-used provision of the IDE
spec regarding the alignment in memory of buffers. How often do you care
about the IDE controller in a box? You just assume the thing works. 

Now was this a bug in QT code? One could claim that, and language lawyers
doubtless will. One could also claim that the history of the PC industry
is adherence to the way the standard implementations work. Both ways, the
point is that here's a weirdo heisenbug only appearing on one weirdo box
that only shows through exhaustive testing of one box after the next.

Maynard



