In article <handleym-1204990813460001@handma3.apple.com>,
	handleym@ricochet.net (Maynard Handley) writes:
> Am I the only person in the world who writes every single damn function he
> types up as 
> 
> #if DEBUG
>     #define FLAG_ERROR(error) if(error!=noErr) Debugger()
> #else
>     #define FLAG_ERROR(error) 
> #endif
> 
>     error=CallSomeFunc(...);
>     FLAG_ERROR(error);
> 
> I have never seen any other code that does this, yet the pain it saves is
> immeasurable. Regardless of whether or not I can do anything useful
> subsequently with the error, the very fact that I catch the problem in the
> debugger at the source, not 50 corrupted memory blocks later is
> extraordinarily useful.

Catching errors early is obviously a good thing, but how is this
mechanism different from using an assert() in the code that is
generating the condition code in the first place?  Of course
that way lies design-by-contract, Eiffel, and who knows:
bug-free code?

I guess your way allows you to decide what is an error condition
in the caller, rather than the callee, but that sounds a bit
like another assertion to me.

Always keen to see a new way to catch bugs, so thanks for that.

-- 
Andrew Reilly



