In article <7eu1cd$ils$1@m2.c2.telstra-mm.net.au> reilly@zeta.org.au wrote:
> In article <handleym-1204990813460001@handma3.apple.com> handleym@ricochet.net
(Maynard Handley) writes:
> > #if DEBUG
> >     #define FLAG_ERROR(error) if(error!=noErr) Debugger()
> > #else
> >     #define FLAG_ERROR(error)
> > #endif
> >
> >     error=CallSomeFunc(...);
> >     FLAG_ERROR(error);
>
> Catching errors early is obviously a good thing, but how is this
> mechanism different from using an assert() in the code that is
> generating the condition code in the first place?

(1) It isn't nececessarily an error, but a condition that some
    callers of CallSomeFunc know how to handle.
(2) CallSomeFunc is a standard library subroutine or otherwise
    separately compiled so that it doesn't signal errors.

BTW - The FLAG_ERROR approach has the benefit that its behavior
doesn't depend on DEBUG when the condition does not hold.  assert()
doesn't work that way.  (To see why, look at what happens with
assert(!CallSomeFunc()).)  assert() is the sort of "design" which
happens when you optimize at the wrong level, in this case because
some "important" compiler might not remove dead-code.

In other words, assert() makes it awkward to write "evaluate, and
if DEBUG is set and an error occurs...."  It only lets you write, "if
DEBUG is set and an error occurs...."  The former can be easily
sprinkled through code, using the latter requires work (and can introduce
bugs), so it doesn't happen.

-andy

