>
>         Given that terminals and graphics monitors don't have any moving
>     parts, most electronics (of this general type) prefers to be left on
>     _always_.  The heating/cooling cycles  are  what cause solder joints
>     and  other connections to deteriorate.  If you were going to be away
>     "many months", and there was some danger of fire, say, with  no  one
>     around  to  notice,  then you might want to power them down.  But if
>     you've got a computer system that's going to be continuously up, you
>     may as well leave the  terminal/monitor  on  as well.  Oh, I suppose
>     cost of electricity could factor in, eh?  :-}
>

Generally I agree with Ken, an additional factor that I became aware of
when I started working here  is that there is a fire risk associated
with terminals left permamently on. Then again, if you buy cheap Wyse
terminals and don't maintain them and leave them in only marginally suitable
conditions, what do you expect.

The guy who used to be really paranoid and force me to
disconnect the console lines then power off the console terminal has moved
on to another project now anyway.

It used to really bug me at the Uni when the security guards would turn off
the monitors. Luckily, they learned quick about turning off the desktop
VMS and Unix boxes.

