In article <7ejo9j$h03$1@news3.microserve.net>, bogus@bogus.com (Stephen Eickhoff) writes:

  I will assume you have followed recommended procedures and shut down
  and powered down the VAXstation system before disconnecting the SCSI 
  bus.  (Failure to do this can potentially corrupt data transfers.)
  I will also assume the SCSI is correctly terminated, and is as short
  as you can make it -- over-long or poor-quality SCSI cables can cause
  serious problems.

:I'm trying to hook up an ancient DEC RRD40 CD-ROM to my 4000-60. 

  The RRD40 is a circa 1X CD-ROM drive, and takes what are now rather
  unusual carriers...

:Although the CD shows up on a PC BIOS scan and works fine, in the VAX
:configuration it doesn't show up with show dev.

  "PC BIOS scan"?  I expect you mean "console".

: I've checked, it doesn't 
:conflict with my hard drive SCSI ID. Do I need to do anything special 
:before boot-up to get it recognized?

  Nope.  Assuming there are no SCSI unit ID collisions -- and unlike a
  Microsoft Windows or Windows NT system -- OpenVMS will automatically 
  detect the appearance of a supported disk device and will then
  autoconfigure the necessary device drivers.

:                                     Also, does anyone have docs for 
:this drive? I'm not doing well trying to guess what the DIP switches do.

  Switches 1, 2, and 3 set the SCSI ID number.  For example,
  SCSI ID is set as Up Down Down.  The position of the fourth 
  switch has no effect -- the default setting is Down.

    ID  1     2     3     4
    --------------------------
    0   Down  Down  Down  Down
    1   Down  Down  Up    Down
    2   Down  Up    Down  Down
    3   Down  Up    Up    Down
    4   Up    Down  Down  Down
    5   Up    Down  Up    Down
    6   Up    Up    Down  Down
    7   Up    Up    Up    Down

