>claird@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM (Cameron Laird) writes:
>
>> Programming is one of the smaller components of the
>> cost of delivering a product to customers.  Even if
>> porting is entirely free from the point of view of
>> development staff, it's shockingly expensive to buy
>> a support department, marketing talent, ... for
>> each new supported platform.
>
>Agreed. The point actually is, is Alpha/NT a "new supported platform" if your
>software is already running on Intel/NT? What application-visible differences
>are there?
>
>	Jan

For one, "running on Intel/NT" is a pretty loose statement...
Just because your code runs on the Intel version of NT doesn't mean
it will run on the Alpha/NT unmodified.  The Intel and Alpha compilers
have different bugs, and as I remember there are various functions/system
calls that were available on Intel, but not on Alpha.  I haven't messed
with Alpha/NT for almost two years though, so things may have improved
since then.  The company I worked for at the time also wanted/needed support
for things on Alpha that just weren't there.  Microsoft doesn't do a very
good job of supporting Alpha as a platform, or at least they weren't back
then.  This was particularly true for people doing digital video work, which
we were...  I remember there being lots and lots of deficiencies in Alpha/NT
/wrt to the availability and/or correct operation of numerous video codecs.
It used to seem like every other API call had a footnote that said
"not supported on Alpha" at the time.  We actually did produce a saleable
product, but it lacked a lot of features compared to the Intel version of
the same product due to poor support from Microsoft and other vendors
(Yes, we wanted Quicktime as well, but it wasn't forthcoming then.  Of
 course, at the time, the Quicktime development libraries for Intel were
 an unmitigated buggy disaster as well.)

I'm glad to be out of all of that mess and back doing Unix development.

  John Stone
