>you would like to use. But given that OS and its utilities are the same, how
>does a normal application see a difference - except possibly in performance -
>whether it is running on a P5-66, a P6-300, a Xeon-450, a 21164-300, or a
>21264-600? Or are you saying it is industry practice to test and validate a
>given product on any hardware variant, even in just the Intel/NT space, say,
>that it might be sold on?


(1) If its a real-time application, yes.

(2) I hope that most applications are not specific to a particular piece of hardware,
CPU, etc.;  but I know of at least one significant piece of software that refused
to run on a processor that had a different cache size. Not because of hardware bugs,
not because of implicit cache size dependencies, but because the piece of code that,
during boot, looked at the cache size the processor reported to it, said "I have never
seen such a cache size before", and panicked. When asked why, the developers
said that they refused to run on any configuration they had not tested, and since
they had never had a processor of that cache size before, they refused to run.

I don't know, but I suspect that the moral here is somethig like "don't tell software
anything it doesn't need to know for semantic correctness - even if it would be nice
to let software know for performance tuning".

:-(






