>> Programming is one of the smaller components of the
>> cost of delivering a product to customers.  Even if
>> porting is entirely free from the point of view of
>> development staff, it's shockingly expensive to buy
>> a support department, marketing talent, ... for
>> each new supported platform.
>
>Agreed. The point actually is, is Alpha/NT a "new supported platform" if your
>software is already running on Intel/NT?

Yes.

> What application-visible differences
>are there?

Compiler bugs.  Bug incompatibilities between code which was written in 
assembler on either or both platform.  OS differences (mapped files behave 
slightly differently on NT for Intel and NT for Alpha).

You can't just compile and forget.  You have to test on both platforms.  
Testing costs money.  You have to field bug reports on both platforms, and 
verify whether they occur on one or both.  Giving all your support engineers 
two machines costs money.  Getting all your engineers to verify each reported 
bug on two platforms costs money.

And so on.....

