In article <370A684A.990A008@rconnect.com>, "Derek J.Hunt" <djhunt@rconnect.com> writes:
:I have recently purchased an old MicroVax 3500 or 3600 and I have
:several questions regarding its operation;
:
:The machine appears to boot properly (i.e. no fault lights or anything),
:but I do not know how to get into console on this.

  The key is the number.  The countdown system should get to roughly 3
  at a minimum (if it passes the self-tests), and possibly as far as 0
  (if it boots).

:  Where would I find a
:console port (for a terminal or modem) on this beast?

  The console port for the KA650 S-box module is on the bulkhead for
  the processor module.  There are two console pinouts used on most of
  the Q-bus VAX and MicroVAX systems -- the older 9-pin pinout (this
  is not used in the BA2xx-series S-box enclosure that you likely have 
  here; also be aware that this pinout predates and differs from the 
  PC 9-pin serial line pinout) and the MMJ modified modular jack pinout.

: It looks like it
:has a DECnet port card 

  There is no such thing as a "DECnet port card".

  Various Q-bus VAX and MicroVAX systems did commonly have DESQA or 
  DELQA Q-bus Ethernet and IEEE 802.3 controllers, and these controllers 
  are capable of running IP, DECnet, LAT, and a variety of other Ethernet 
  (or IEEE 802.3) protocols.

:on it with an array of LEDs that display a number
:or letter on bootup; It has an ethernet card in it and two or four SCSI
:cards in it;  but I don't see an RS232 port or anything close to that;

  There are usually "M" numbers on the card spines or on the Q-bus
  bulkheads of DIGITAL Q-bus cards, and these will identify the
  particular card.  For example, the DELQA was assigned the M7516 
  module number, and the KA650 processor module was assigned M7620.
  With a list of these, we can tell you what is in the enclosure.

:Is there a place on the web that has some technical information about
:this beast or some functional schematics?  

  Visit http://www.dejanews.com/, search for some combination of
  "comp.os.vms, hoffman, and serpentine".  One of the postings that
  this will return will describe the serpentine layout scheme used 
  on the Q-bus, something you need to know.

:If anyone has any ideas on
:how I can get into this crazy machine please respond via E-mail or the
:newsgroup.

  Please see the OpenVMS Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ) for pointers to 
  the pinout of the MMJ connector, for instructions on getting into OpenVMS 
  if you don't know the SYSTEM password, for hobbyist OpenVMS license 
  information, and for pointers to a variety of hardware-related websites 
  around the 'net.

  The OpenVMS FAQ is available via a link at:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/

