Joe Mario <mario@zk3.dec.com> writes:

>Andrew Gaylard wrote:
>> 
>> Hi all,
>> 
>> I have a very puzzling error, which our DEC support people haven't
>> been able to figure out.  It looks like this:
>> 
>> {root}~: netstat -r
>>  no namelist
>>
>> So I can't get a routing table from Unix (or anything else for that
>> matter):
>> 
>> {root}~: netstat -i
>>  no namelist
> 
>The problem is that your /sbin/kloadsrv daemon isn't running.
>It can be restarted with 

>	/sbin/kloadsrv < /dev/console > /dev/console 2>&1

>That syntax exists in the /etc/inittab file, in case you forget it.

>The real question is why did /sbin/kloadsrv crash?  You don't
>mention what release you're running.  There was a
>known problem in /vmunix that was causing kloadsrv to randomly
>crash.  That problem was fixed as of the V4.0E release and is
>fixed in the support/patch releases for V4.0A -> V4.0D.

OK, that fixed it!  Thanks to all those who replied; my faith in the 
'net is restored!

The problem was that in ASE, when a machine boots, the 'stop' script 
for each service is run.  This doesn't make a lot of sense, since the 
services are *obviously* stopped if the machine's busy booting.

Anyway, in one of our ASE 'stop' scripts, we do a /usr/etc/cmi_shutdown
This seems to blow away all kinds of stuff, and indeed, running 
dxdecnetsetup after that produces all kind of scary effects, such as
the above.

The secret is not to run /usr/etc/cmi_shutdown until there's been a
corresponding /usr/etc/cmi_startup first.  To me, this is a bug.
It should not be possible to confuse the kernel to a state where
a reboot is needed. Fortunately, there's a workaround, i.e. just
be careful.  

Thanks all,
--
