In article <36F46F88.A1DE3ABF@clear.net.nz>, thomas_wernitz@clear.net.nz 
says...
>
>Hi people,
>
>I read somewhere on usenet that it is possible to use PC 101 keyboard 
on
>a vt420 terminal.
>I got a vt420 but not the keyboard. Can anyone tell me whether this is
>possible at all, and maybe the pinout of the RJ11 (4pin) jack for the
>DEC keyboard?

As far as I know, the answer is no.  The VT400 series uses the same 
interface as the VT200, VT300 series terminals.

The pinout is on the NET under either the PRO350 FAQ or DECstation or 
Vaxstation 3100.  It has a transmit, receive, grounf and +12 VDC.

A PC keyboard has a clock, bidirectional data, ground and +5 vdc.

You have both data format (async verses sync) and scan code format
to deal with.

The VT510, VT520 use PC keyboards.

BTW it should be trival to hook a DEC LK201 up to a PC serial port...
Just need software, its at 4800 bps.

Max
