When Xerox sold off this division to Honeywell, their user group (Exchange)
merged into HLSUA (Honeywell Large Systems Users' Association).
Names, addresses, & phone #s follow in 2 more screens.

From the '70s until the early '90s, HLSUA was one of the great  users'
groups, comparable to DECUS or SHARE.
The base was actually the General Electric group (the GCOS OS)
and Honeywell kept buying technologies & companies so we had a marvelously 
diverse group.  (CP-6, GCOS, Multics, UNIX, ACOS from NEC,...)

I don't rmember the timing so I'm not sure how much of the CP-V influence
got into UNIX before it was born as the "grandson" of GCOS.  

CP-V evolved into CP-6 and the old Exchange guys and gals were excellent.
They kept the OS and applications growing and extending as the hardware
was replaced by Honeywell and then NEC.

Bull in Europe bought Honeywell's computer division with NEC still
a partner in much of the technology.   After the Bull purchase, HLSUA
changed its name to BUS (Bull Users' Society) [1992?]

I had lots of admiration and respect for the Exchange / CP-6 guys.
They brought us some great technologies.

I was at St. Louis University  (the Jesuit school in St. Louis, MO.)
and we considered a CP-6 system for library automation.


