In article <7e1162$alo$1@panix7.panix.com>, tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:
|> In article <7e0nai$8ov$3@nntp.Stanford.EDU>,
|> Jonathan Stone <jonathan@DSG.Stanford.EDU> wrote:
|> >In article <7dflm7$bkp$1@panix7.panix.com>, tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot Simon) writes:

|> A) I had PX and SFB mixed up.  From various people's comments I thought
|>    I understood both their accellerations for X to be very TGA-like;
|>    is that wrong?  Are the two cards not related?

Not really. The PX (aka PMAG-C) has more in common with the
PMAG-D/E/F.  which have i860s for 2-D and 3-D acceleration.  AFAIK,
none of those boards allow direct access to a raster array; all
rendering is done by writing command packets into a shared-memory
buffer which is read by the on-card `smarts', which interrupts when
it's done.  The higher-end cards do 3D acceleration as well as the 2D
blit ops of the PixelStamp chip.

The TGA and SFB are conceptually similar.



|> B) I was referring to Xserver support for the sfb and PX cards on the pmax,
|>    and the work on getting the TGA cards usable as consoles (as opposed
|>    to "barely functional, because so slow" or "Just Doesn't Work" for a
|>    few models) on the Alpha, as well as the effort to resuscitate the
|>    TGA Xserver.


Ah. For the second part: At least *some* of the problems with TGA
console code was due to horribly mistuned blit code in the
Berkeley-derived rcons code, going thru a bus adaptor that was
essentially half-duplex. Turning the buffer around for each byte or
word was *really* slow.

Someone is working on using the TGA accelerator to do blitting
onboard, which is orders of magnitude faster.  Andy Doran has also
reworked the rcons blit code to use (software) stamps, giving a factor
of about 10 speedup.

Re xservers:, Andy has obtained X11R5 code for the PMAG-C, which
somebody once contributed to MIT. I dont know if the SFB is close
enough to the TGA to reuse or duplicate much of the code, or how that
compares to the highly-tuned code referred to in the DEC WRL techreport.

For the i860-based boards on pmaxes, we may be stuck with upgrading
the Xserver/kernel API from antique DEC-vendor-specific API (which
dates back to the QVSS) to the R5/R6 API, and running Ultrix product
servers using Ultrix emulation.



